WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:04.680
Welcome to "Ear Biscuits",

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.414
the podcast where two lifelong
friends talk about life

00:00:07.414 --> 00:00:08.607
for a long time.

00:00:08.607 --> 00:00:10.390
I'm Rhett.
And I'm Link.

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:13.609
This week at the
roundtable of dim lighting,

00:00:13.609 --> 00:00:16.048
we're having a little listening party.

00:00:16.048 --> 00:00:17.965
Congratulations, Rhett!

00:00:19.749 --> 00:00:21.268
It's finally happened.

00:00:21.268 --> 00:00:23.741
Your freaking album is out!

00:00:23.741 --> 00:00:25.838
I mean, not when we're recording this,

00:00:25.838 --> 00:00:28.094
but yes, when you're listening.

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:30.579
I'm in celebration mode.

00:00:30.579 --> 00:00:33.880
James and the Shame, Rhett's alter ego,

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:37.047
where he plays music that is sincerely

00:00:40.382 --> 00:00:43.890
sentimentized and sincerely great.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:45.089
Oh, thanks, man.

00:00:45.089 --> 00:00:47.397
It's my pleasure to sit here
and listen to it with you

00:00:47.397 --> 00:00:49.794
and kinda talk through
each song, you know?

00:00:49.794 --> 00:00:51.192
Yeah, this is like a listening party.

00:00:51.192 --> 00:00:53.996
I'm gonna take, you know,

00:00:53.996 --> 00:00:55.766
a journalistic approach at times.

00:00:55.766 --> 00:00:57.612
Okay, NPR kinda thing?

00:00:57.612 --> 00:00:59.350
I have a notepad here-

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:00.526
Well, that's intimidating.

00:01:00.526 --> 00:01:04.591
Whereas I'm going to jot,
maybe make some jottings

00:01:04.591 --> 00:01:09.008
that then will lead to you
exposing the truth behind-

00:01:11.294 --> 00:01:14.479
Whoa.
Behind these songs.

00:01:14.479 --> 00:01:15.545
But also-

00:01:15.545 --> 00:01:18.848
I just got all the music
off of a royalty free site

00:01:18.848 --> 00:01:20.995
and just sing over it?

00:01:20.995 --> 00:01:22.702
You want me to admit that finally?

00:01:22.702 --> 00:01:25.785
What if-
Okay, up first. Go for it.

00:01:27.054 --> 00:01:27.887
That would be hilarious.

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:31.550
Again, the album is "Human Overboard".

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:33.779
The whole album is out now

00:01:33.779 --> 00:01:35.884
wherever you listen to your music.

00:01:35.884 --> 00:01:38.486
What we're gonna be doing is
listening to all 11 tracks

00:01:38.486 --> 00:01:39.736
in album order.

00:01:43.098 --> 00:01:44.011
As you're listening to this,

00:01:44.011 --> 00:01:45.160
you're not gonna hear the full track,

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.225
'cause we're not gonna
just play the full song,

00:01:47.225 --> 00:01:48.423
for every single song.

00:01:48.423 --> 00:01:49.620
We're gonna listen to the full song.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.263
So, we might reference something

00:01:52.263 --> 00:01:54.614
that is in one of the full
songs that you didn't hear

00:01:54.614 --> 00:01:56.153
because we're gonna play
like a portion of the song

00:01:56.153 --> 00:01:57.416
to give you the feel and the vibe

00:01:57.416 --> 00:02:01.416
so you can listen to the
album on your own time.

00:02:02.291 --> 00:02:04.291
So that's what we'll do.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:07.719
I'm actually gonna take these out

00:02:07.719 --> 00:02:09.846
'cause I'm actually not
listening to anything right now

00:02:09.846 --> 00:02:10.970
and it makes my voice sound weird.

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:13.189
Yeah, I already had to do that.

00:02:13.189 --> 00:02:17.393
I'm gonna do what they used
to do in the old movies

00:02:17.393 --> 00:02:20.674
and I'm gonna do the credits first.

00:02:20.674 --> 00:02:21.581
'Cause I don't wanna just put

00:02:21.581 --> 00:02:23.513
the people that I wanna think at the end,

00:02:23.513 --> 00:02:24.467
and you're just like,

00:02:24.467 --> 00:02:26.107
"I've heard the whole
album and then I'm done."

00:02:26.107 --> 00:02:27.556
And so you might wanna fast,

00:02:27.556 --> 00:02:29.210
I mean, if you decide
you wanna fast-forward-

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:30.421
Play fast-forward through it?

00:02:30.421 --> 00:02:31.282
No, I'm saying you might decide

00:02:31.282 --> 00:02:32.412
that you wanna fast-forward, but don't.

00:02:32.412 --> 00:02:37.412
Because one of the coolest
things about this whole project

00:02:37.566 --> 00:02:40.234
has been the collaborative nature

00:02:40.234 --> 00:02:44.120
of it coming to fruition
and being what it is.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:45.791
You know, I had a lot of people

00:02:45.791 --> 00:02:48.958
who made me sound the way that I sound

00:02:50.088 --> 00:02:55.022
and shaped these songs and
shaped the whole project

00:02:55.022 --> 00:02:57.106
that I collaborated with
on different parts of it,

00:02:57.106 --> 00:03:00.356
so I wanna just start by thanking them.

00:03:02.542 --> 00:03:04.440
Do you have a list?
I do.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:05.970
First of all, I wanna just start with-

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.107
Now you haven't won an
award, I just wanna clarify.

00:03:08.107 --> 00:03:09.836
Yeah, no, I haven't.

00:03:09.836 --> 00:03:12.052
Maybe you will.

00:03:12.052 --> 00:03:13.503
Well, okay. What kind of award?

00:03:14.581 --> 00:03:15.952
I'm just saying, typically
you thank people-

00:03:15.952 --> 00:03:20.417
The Daily Record is gonna
be, "Best album of the year

00:03:20.417 --> 00:03:24.750
from a guy who used to have
a studio in Lillington."

00:03:25.873 --> 00:03:27.410
Yeah, you could get

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:30.525
a Chamber of Commerce award
at least for this thing.

00:03:30.525 --> 00:03:31.861
No, I'm just saying these are the people

00:03:31.861 --> 00:03:33.717
who made this project what it is.

00:03:33.717 --> 00:03:34.791
Shout 'em out, man.

00:03:34.791 --> 00:03:38.065
So, first of all, the person
who played the biggest role,

00:03:38.065 --> 00:03:41.900
Derek Fuhrmann, my producer,
who of course we got to know-

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.433
I love me some Derek.

00:03:43.433 --> 00:03:46.723
We got to know through
Britain, Link's cousin,

00:03:46.723 --> 00:03:48.248
because he was recording
some stuff with him

00:03:48.248 --> 00:03:50.063
and he's done some stuff
from the Mythical Society,

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:52.244
for, you know, our collabs
that we do over there

00:03:52.244 --> 00:03:53.568
at the vinyl that we do.

00:03:53.568 --> 00:03:56.903
And, you know, the reason I decided to go

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:59.008
with Derek on this project

00:03:59.008 --> 00:04:02.061
isn't because he had never
done any country or folk music,

00:04:02.061 --> 00:04:03.838
he's a pop guy,

00:04:03.838 --> 00:04:08.171
but because he is so committed
to making things work

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.679
and he was so committed
to learning the process.

00:04:11.679 --> 00:04:14.182
And, like, he told me
multiple things like,

00:04:14.182 --> 00:04:17.369
"I'm learning so much
about this type of music,"

00:04:17.369 --> 00:04:20.058
you know, as we make these songs.

00:04:20.058 --> 00:04:21.340
And, again, the way that it worked

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:25.586
is I would write the lyrics and the music,

00:04:25.586 --> 00:04:27.828
basically me and a guitar,

00:04:27.828 --> 00:04:30.828
singing to a certain beat, that bpm.

00:04:32.046 --> 00:04:33.283
That's beat per minute.

00:04:33.283 --> 00:04:34.809
Yeah, and I would send him that

00:04:34.809 --> 00:04:38.916
and that is the backbone of
everything that he then create.

00:04:38.916 --> 00:04:40.771
If it was one beat per
minute, that would be slow.

00:04:40.771 --> 00:04:44.325
Sorry, go ahead.
That's whale music.

00:04:44.325 --> 00:04:47.729
But then he would kinda send back,

00:04:47.729 --> 00:04:49.998
because, again, I have another job,

00:04:49.998 --> 00:04:52.351
actually several other jobs
that I do here at Mythical.

00:04:52.351 --> 00:04:54.782
So, this has been a
side project and a hobby

00:04:54.782 --> 00:04:56.387
so I haven't been able
to devote the kinda time

00:04:56.387 --> 00:05:00.470
that typically people
devote to this kinda thing.

00:05:01.345 --> 00:05:03.363
So, I was send him these demos

00:05:03.363 --> 00:05:05.359
and then he would come
back with, like, he's like,

00:05:05.359 --> 00:05:07.563
"Hey, here's what I'm
thinking about the baseline.

00:05:07.563 --> 00:05:09.709
This is kinda what I'm
hearing in a drum groove,"

00:05:09.709 --> 00:05:12.139
kinda thing that then we would
then send off to other people

00:05:12.139 --> 00:05:13.251
to add their pieces.

00:05:13.251 --> 00:05:16.549
It was like so collaborative
and so many steps

00:05:16.549 --> 00:05:18.614
to finally get to the final song.

00:05:18.614 --> 00:05:22.002
But he was the one that
was guiding that process

00:05:22.002 --> 00:05:23.646
to kinda be like, "Here's what,"

00:05:23.646 --> 00:05:26.058
he would send me a bunch of
options and he would send me,

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:29.276
but really it was his ear and his instinct

00:05:29.276 --> 00:05:31.475
with these different
musicians that he hired

00:05:31.475 --> 00:05:32.909
that made these songs go

00:05:32.909 --> 00:05:36.457
from a guy playing something on a guitar

00:05:36.457 --> 00:05:38.905
to what you end up hearing on the album.

00:05:38.905 --> 00:05:41.818
And he was just so intimately involved

00:05:41.818 --> 00:05:42.987
in making it what it is.

00:05:42.987 --> 00:05:46.743
So, I just wanted to start,
big thanks to Derek Fuhrmann.

00:05:46.743 --> 00:05:48.441
You would be lucky to
have him as a producer

00:05:48.441 --> 00:05:50.165
on your project.

00:05:50.165 --> 00:05:52.376
He stays very busy but-

00:05:52.376 --> 00:05:54.061
You know, if you've not experienced it,

00:05:54.061 --> 00:05:55.499
sometimes it's hard.

00:05:55.499 --> 00:05:57.970
Well, in hip hop it's actually, you know,

00:05:57.970 --> 00:05:59.996
a lot more credit is given to producers.

00:05:59.996 --> 00:06:02.084
But, like, growing up listening to music,

00:06:02.084 --> 00:06:05.705
I really didn't think
about the producer's role.

00:06:05.705 --> 00:06:07.310
And so everything you just described

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:09.960
is not necessarily something

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:13.127
that is just because of your situation

00:06:14.992 --> 00:06:17.343
and all the different directions
that you've been pulled in

00:06:17.343 --> 00:06:19.565
that you needed to, like,

00:06:19.565 --> 00:06:20.888
give it over to Derek or something,

00:06:20.888 --> 00:06:23.437
I think you're describing
what a good producer does,

00:06:23.437 --> 00:06:25.167
which is, you know,

00:06:25.167 --> 00:06:27.388
something that I didn't
appreciate at first

00:06:27.388 --> 00:06:30.312
and, like, helping
bring shape and cohesion

00:06:30.312 --> 00:06:32.895
and specific direction to songs

00:06:34.106 --> 00:06:36.870
that are in their rawest form, you know?

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:40.630
A producer can make or
break a song entirely

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:42.585
with where he sees it.

00:06:42.585 --> 00:06:45.015
A great producer is
also a musical director,

00:06:45.015 --> 00:06:48.101
somebody who's like, "Hey,
let's try that again,"

00:06:48.101 --> 00:06:50.927
and, "Give me a little bit more softness,"

00:06:50.927 --> 00:06:53.951
or, "Give me a little bit
more emotion in that,"

00:06:53.951 --> 00:06:57.066
or, you know, even a couple of times,

00:06:57.066 --> 00:06:57.966
this didn't happen on every song,

00:06:57.966 --> 00:06:59.883
but there were a couple of
songs where he was like,

00:06:59.883 --> 00:07:03.365
"Ah, the phrasing of that
part is kinda bumping me,"

00:07:03.365 --> 00:07:05.251
and we would sit there and we
would try to figure out like,

00:07:05.251 --> 00:07:07.544
"Oh, I'm gonna use this syllable.

00:07:07.544 --> 00:07:11.319
I'm gonna use this word here,"
and, like, locking it in

00:07:11.319 --> 00:07:13.863
so there's not things that
bump you throughout the album.

00:07:13.863 --> 00:07:15.760
So, he was integral in that process.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:19.593
Another person that did
so much, Alex Strahle.

00:07:20.654 --> 00:07:22.379
The first time I talked about Alex,

00:07:22.379 --> 00:07:24.463
when we talked about "Believe Me",

00:07:24.463 --> 00:07:25.493
I mispronounced his last name.

00:07:25.493 --> 00:07:28.076
I think I said Strohl. Strahle.

00:07:28.977 --> 00:07:33.913
So, Alex is just a, he's a
multi-instrumentalist musician

00:07:33.913 --> 00:07:37.275
here in California, in Los Angeles,

00:07:37.275 --> 00:07:38.338
who's in a couple of bands,

00:07:38.338 --> 00:07:41.422
but he's also kind of
a session guitar guy.

00:07:41.422 --> 00:07:43.005
And Derek knew him.

00:07:44.584 --> 00:07:47.091
You know, originally, with so much,

00:07:47.091 --> 00:07:48.647
like, we wanted to do pedal steel,

00:07:48.647 --> 00:07:50.829
we wanted to do obviously,

00:07:50.829 --> 00:07:53.494
like, country sounding guitar in places,

00:07:53.494 --> 00:07:56.494
we wanted banjo, we wanted mandolin.

00:07:57.999 --> 00:07:59.422
Alex did all of that.

00:07:59.422 --> 00:08:01.472
So, originally it was gonna be like,

00:08:01.472 --> 00:08:03.816
Derek was like, "I gotta
have to talk to some contacts

00:08:03.816 --> 00:08:05.572
to get some Nashville guys

00:08:05.572 --> 00:08:08.449
to do these authentic country parts."

00:08:08.449 --> 00:08:12.232
And then he didn't have
any luck with that,

00:08:12.232 --> 00:08:15.476
but he is like, "Hey, I
know a guy in Pasadena.

00:08:15.476 --> 00:08:16.643
Alex Strahle."

00:08:17.999 --> 00:08:20.356
He's got all these old
guitars and old instruments.

00:08:20.356 --> 00:08:24.374
Like, the pedal steel
is a 1946 instrument.

00:08:24.374 --> 00:08:25.235
Wow.

00:08:25.235 --> 00:08:26.430
That's not even a pedal steel.

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:28.358
It's a multi-cord.

00:08:28.358 --> 00:08:30.943
It is like a predecessor to a pedal steel-

00:08:30.943 --> 00:08:31.928
Really?

00:08:31.928 --> 00:08:35.496
That he engineered to be able to,

00:08:35.496 --> 00:08:37.629
because basically the way the multi-cord,

00:08:37.629 --> 00:08:39.848
you would hit a pedal and
it changes the whole cord

00:08:39.848 --> 00:08:41.783
that's being played, right?

00:08:41.783 --> 00:08:43.537
He found a way to make it where he can,

00:08:43.537 --> 00:08:45.543
he, like, does all this,

00:08:45.543 --> 00:08:47.310
he manipulates all these
instruments and stuff

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:49.480
and he basically engineered this thing

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:50.675
to make it into a pedal steel.

00:08:50.675 --> 00:08:52.820
But that's not technically
what you would see

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:54.494
as a lap pedal steel.

00:08:54.494 --> 00:08:56.138
Oh, a pedal steel.

00:08:56.138 --> 00:08:57.861
You play the cords with your feet?

00:08:57.861 --> 00:08:59.575
With your feet. A pedal.

00:08:59.575 --> 00:09:00.806
How many pedals are there?

00:09:00.806 --> 00:09:02.196
Is it like an organ?

00:09:02.196 --> 00:09:04.405
I don't know.
What?

00:09:04.405 --> 00:09:05.552
I haven't seen it.

00:09:05.552 --> 00:09:07.038
Yeah, because when they're playing,

00:09:07.038 --> 00:09:08.166
they're striking-

00:09:08.166 --> 00:09:11.503
These were remote, in some senses,

00:09:11.503 --> 00:09:14.749
I think, yeah, they're,
like, on a live session

00:09:14.749 --> 00:09:16.349
with each other that I could tune into

00:09:16.349 --> 00:09:17.374
while I was doing my job.

00:09:17.374 --> 00:09:20.866
But I wasn't there when they're
actually doing it, right?

00:09:20.866 --> 00:09:24.324
Did you ever meet Alex in person at all?

00:09:24.324 --> 00:09:28.069
By the time this podcast
is being recorded I will,

00:09:28.069 --> 00:09:30.456
because-
No, we are recording it.

00:09:30.456 --> 00:09:32.635
Podcast is live because I wanted to go out

00:09:32.635 --> 00:09:34.921
with Derek and Alex
because they were the two,

00:09:34.921 --> 00:09:36.565
like, you know, they're also-

00:09:36.565 --> 00:09:37.750
So, you have plans to meet him,

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:39.228
but you still have not met him?

00:09:39.228 --> 00:09:42.128
I wanna do a celebration,
have dinner with them.

00:09:42.128 --> 00:09:44.427
So yes, we will have done that.

00:09:44.427 --> 00:09:47.219
But anyway, Alex is, again,
he's not a country guy.

00:09:47.219 --> 00:09:49.018
He just has great instincts.

00:09:49.018 --> 00:09:50.542
And I think it ended up bringing

00:09:50.542 --> 00:09:53.746
this awesome vibe to a lot of the,

00:09:53.746 --> 00:09:55.555
so the way that I would describe it

00:09:55.555 --> 00:09:58.012
is if you are impressed by guitar

00:09:58.012 --> 00:10:02.939
that you hear on the album,
it's him, not me, right?

00:10:02.939 --> 00:10:04.707
You know, I'm proficient,

00:10:04.707 --> 00:10:07.164
especially as a songwriter
when it comes to,

00:10:07.164 --> 00:10:09.633
I can do what I need to do
on a guitar to write a song

00:10:09.633 --> 00:10:11.985
and to kinda keep rhythm
and do a picking pattern

00:10:11.985 --> 00:10:13.480
and that kinda thing.

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:15.087
But, like, if you're impressed by it,

00:10:15.087 --> 00:10:16.870
chances are it was him doing it.

00:10:16.870 --> 00:10:18.631
All right, who else you got?

00:10:18.631 --> 00:10:22.013
Gunnar Olsen did all the
percussion and all the drums.

00:10:22.013 --> 00:10:23.818
That's a good name for
someone to do somethin'.

00:10:23.818 --> 00:10:24.773
And this is just a dude

00:10:24.773 --> 00:10:28.541
who's a session drummer in New York,

00:10:28.541 --> 00:10:30.346
who has worked with a bunch of big stars.

00:10:30.346 --> 00:10:34.021
He did all the percussion on Springsteen's

00:10:34.021 --> 00:10:35.699
"Western Stars" album.

00:10:35.699 --> 00:10:38.127
He's worked with Miley
Cyrus and, you know,

00:10:38.127 --> 00:10:39.421
a bunch of people who are more talented

00:10:39.421 --> 00:10:41.771
and more prolific than me.

00:10:41.771 --> 00:10:44.316
But, again, Derek knows him

00:10:44.316 --> 00:10:48.941
and he knew he could do this
kind of, like, Americana style

00:10:48.941 --> 00:10:49.832
and he nailed it.

00:10:49.832 --> 00:10:51.254
So, thanks to Gunnar.

00:10:51.254 --> 00:10:52.632
I'll talk about more musicians

00:10:52.632 --> 00:10:54.130
once we get into the individual tracks.

00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:56.699
But, again, I wanna think Anna Weber,

00:10:56.699 --> 00:10:58.889
who took all the pictures
for the whole thing.

00:10:58.889 --> 00:11:00.650
So, any pictures where I look

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:03.837
like I might be a legitimate artist-

00:11:03.837 --> 00:11:06.054
Or feeling shame.
Yes.

00:11:06.054 --> 00:11:07.249
While the picture's being taken.

00:11:07.249 --> 00:11:09.920
That was Anna Weber. Thanks to Anna Weber.

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:12.507
The album art for the album,

00:11:12.507 --> 00:11:15.385
you know, the me in
the Water on the front,

00:11:15.385 --> 00:11:19.789
that was illustrator Greg
Newbold who painted that,

00:11:19.789 --> 00:11:21.595
digitally painted that.

00:11:21.595 --> 00:11:23.036
And I don't think he's
done much album art.

00:11:23.036 --> 00:11:25.326
He's just like a legit,
like, artist/painter

00:11:25.326 --> 00:11:29.060
who does, like, gallery
work and stuff and-

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:30.135
That's cool.

00:11:30.135 --> 00:11:31.900
You know, Cara helped me find him online

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:34.612
and he did an incredible job.

00:11:34.612 --> 00:11:36.675
Kendrick Kidd, who we used to work with

00:11:36.675 --> 00:11:37.869
a long time ago, do a lot of-

00:11:37.869 --> 00:11:39.188
Back in the day.
design.

00:11:39.188 --> 00:11:41.613
He designed the James and the Shame logo.

00:11:41.613 --> 00:11:45.113
And then Cara, Cara Powers, our assistant-

00:11:46.306 --> 00:11:49.546
Kendrick designed the first
Good Mythical Morning logo.

00:11:49.546 --> 00:11:50.645
The flame.
Yeah, yeah.

00:11:50.645 --> 00:11:51.590
Yeah, see? Yeah.

00:11:51.590 --> 00:11:54.923
And the Rhett &amp; Link logo that has us,

00:11:56.223 --> 00:11:59.174
we look like one chess
piece, like two faces facing-

00:11:59.174 --> 00:12:01.468
Yeah, the two heads, the
one head and two faces.

00:12:01.468 --> 00:12:03.692
Yeah, he also did that.

00:12:03.692 --> 00:12:04.886
Yeah.

00:12:04.886 --> 00:12:06.569
And then Cara Powers, our assistant,

00:12:06.569 --> 00:12:09.522
who spent a lot of her time
working on this with me

00:12:09.522 --> 00:12:11.862
in finding these talented people.

00:12:11.862 --> 00:12:15.112
So, it was just so cool because I just,

00:12:16.237 --> 00:12:18.694
you know, we haven't done
anything like this in a while

00:12:18.694 --> 00:12:21.524
because we have this incredible
team here at Mythical

00:12:21.524 --> 00:12:26.369
that we have an idea, and the
team implements that idea,

00:12:26.369 --> 00:12:28.663
but this was kinda more the
old school way we used to work,

00:12:28.663 --> 00:12:29.933
which is like, "Hey, we need somebody.

00:12:29.933 --> 00:12:32.139
Let's find somebody online
that we like their work

00:12:32.139 --> 00:12:34.687
and we can just collaborate with them."

00:12:34.687 --> 00:12:35.520
It was really cool

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:36.997
just when you bring
somebody into a process

00:12:36.997 --> 00:12:40.330
and they make something into what it is.

00:12:41.423 --> 00:12:42.677
Well, without further ado,

00:12:42.677 --> 00:12:43.831
we can start listening to the song.

00:12:43.831 --> 00:12:47.221
But you know what, I might
as well just talk quickly

00:12:47.221 --> 00:12:48.741
so we don't break up the album.

00:12:48.741 --> 00:12:50.347
Let's talk about We're Still Good.

00:12:50.347 --> 00:12:51.368
We're Still Good.

00:12:51.368 --> 00:12:52.445
We're Still Good.

00:12:52.445 --> 00:12:56.380
This is the party game from Mythical.

00:12:56.380 --> 00:13:00.028
For all of your friends to get together.

00:13:00.028 --> 00:13:01.641
You play a card.

00:13:01.641 --> 00:13:04.819
It has a blank, it's a crazy scenario

00:13:04.819 --> 00:13:06.441
that then everybody submits

00:13:06.441 --> 00:13:08.083
what they think should fill in the blank,

00:13:08.083 --> 00:13:12.357
person chooses what makes
that scenario horrible,

00:13:12.357 --> 00:13:15.978
and then everybody needs to
write a positive spin saying

00:13:15.978 --> 00:13:19.459
"We're still good" to turn
it into something positive.

00:13:19.459 --> 00:13:21.151
So it's like-
It's a very fulfilling game.

00:13:21.151 --> 00:13:22.175
It's good for your soul.

00:13:22.175 --> 00:13:23.670
It'll make you laugh.

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:26.873
It'll also help you reframe things,

00:13:26.873 --> 00:13:29.812
if you're into that therapy.

00:13:29.812 --> 00:13:31.568
Which is important. I
think it's important.

00:13:31.568 --> 00:13:34.138
Reframing. Language is important.

00:13:34.138 --> 00:13:35.853
Stoic philosophy.

00:13:35.853 --> 00:13:37.523
Yeah, we heard from a therapist

00:13:37.523 --> 00:13:41.908
that said they were
using it in that context.

00:13:41.908 --> 00:13:42.980
We're Still Good.

00:13:42.980 --> 00:13:44.615
Available at amazon.com.

00:13:44.615 --> 00:13:46.351
It took me a while to figure out

00:13:46.351 --> 00:13:48.276
what the album order is going to be.

00:13:48.276 --> 00:13:49.898
And actually this process right now

00:13:49.898 --> 00:13:52.958
is committing me to the album order.

00:13:52.958 --> 00:13:54.617
Oh, okay.

00:13:54.617 --> 00:13:55.643
Well, okay.

00:13:55.643 --> 00:13:57.018
Yeah, I gave some input into this,

00:13:57.018 --> 00:13:58.432
so I'm curious how it's gonna shake out.

00:13:58.432 --> 00:13:59.622
I uploaded them.

00:13:59.622 --> 00:14:00.760
I uploaded the songs yesterday.

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:01.612
I could technically-

00:14:01.612 --> 00:14:05.886
I have not listened to them
in album order before now.

00:14:05.886 --> 00:14:07.839
"Believe Me", the first
single, has been out.

00:14:07.839 --> 00:14:08.993
We've already listened to it once,

00:14:08.993 --> 00:14:11.930
but we gotta listen to it again.

00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:13.125
All right, here we go.

00:14:31.367 --> 00:14:36.367
♪ I think you want an answer ♪

00:14:38.008 --> 00:14:43.008
♪ I'm not prepared to give ♪

00:14:43.907 --> 00:14:48.262
♪ 'Cause the one I gave you said that ♪

00:14:48.262 --> 00:14:53.262
♪ That ain't it ♪

00:14:55.065 --> 00:15:00.065
♪ Must be something that I want ♪

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:06.550
♪ Fame and fortune or at
least a little jaunt ♪

00:15:12.722 --> 00:15:17.722
♪ It may seem too cut and dried ♪

00:15:19.354 --> 00:15:21.891
♪ But I just found some things ♪

00:15:21.891 --> 00:15:26.891
♪ I could not brush aside ♪

00:15:27.732 --> 00:15:32.444
♪ Now if you'll oblige ♪

00:15:32.444 --> 00:15:37.444
♪ I don't think it's true ♪

00:15:42.161 --> 00:15:46.408
♪ I'm not asking you to agree ♪

00:15:46.408 --> 00:15:50.741
♪ I'm just asking you to believe me ♪

00:16:00.183 --> 00:16:04.860
♪ You say my heart was never true ♪

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:08.860
♪ That might say more about you ♪

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:14.732
Love it.

00:16:14.732 --> 00:16:16.132
First single.

00:16:16.132 --> 00:16:18.883
You know, we've already
talked about this one

00:16:18.883 --> 00:16:20.278
when the single came out,

00:16:20.278 --> 00:16:23.552
so I don't wanna retread
any of that ground.

00:16:23.552 --> 00:16:26.260
But we have the benefit of, like,

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:30.239
you've heard the most fan
feedback from this song

00:16:30.239 --> 00:16:33.703
because it's been out
the longest, you know.

00:16:33.703 --> 00:16:36.167
So, one I'm kinda curious about,

00:16:36.167 --> 00:16:40.100
like, what's that experience been like?

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:41.933
Are people getting it?

00:16:43.242 --> 00:16:45.596
Are people, you know, what is it,

00:16:45.596 --> 00:16:49.013
what kinda conversation is it leading to?

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:53.098
Because most of the
people who are listening,

00:16:53.098 --> 00:16:54.450
at this point, listening to my music

00:16:54.450 --> 00:16:57.617
are people who are already fans of us.

00:16:59.108 --> 00:17:03.160
We're getting, like, the
reaction to this type of music

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:05.731
from people who are just fans of us,

00:17:05.731 --> 00:17:08.611
which doesn't mean they're
fans of country music.

00:17:08.611 --> 00:17:10.759
Now, we have a subset of Mythical Beasts

00:17:10.759 --> 00:17:14.202
who are fans of country
music, but the most common,

00:17:14.202 --> 00:17:17.009
and, first of all, the
response has been so positive

00:17:17.009 --> 00:17:19.358
from a musical standpoint.

00:17:19.358 --> 00:17:21.755
I'll talk about, you know,
thematically that response.

00:17:21.755 --> 00:17:22.838
But surprise.

00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:26.994
You know, people are like,
"Oh," you know, "I didn't,"

00:17:26.994 --> 00:17:29.096
people do this kinda
thing all the time, right,

00:17:29.096 --> 00:17:30.010
and it's a trope.

00:17:30.010 --> 00:17:31.890
It's a trope for if you're

00:17:31.890 --> 00:17:33.629
doing this one thing in
entertainment, you're like,

00:17:33.629 --> 00:17:37.519
you always just think, "I
can also do music," you know.

00:17:37.519 --> 00:17:40.005
I think the thing that
is a reminder to people-

00:17:40.005 --> 00:17:42.277
I think podcast is the new
version of that would be, yeah.

00:17:42.277 --> 00:17:43.453
Yeah, yeah.

00:17:43.453 --> 00:17:45.983
Is that we started our
career as musicians.

00:17:45.983 --> 00:17:48.429
So, this is going back
to the basics for me

00:17:48.429 --> 00:17:49.295
in a lot of ways.

00:17:49.295 --> 00:17:52.249
The first thing we ever did
professionally as entertainers,

00:17:52.249 --> 00:17:54.166
it was music based, you know.

00:17:54.166 --> 00:17:55.865
But people are still
surprised because, you know,

00:17:55.865 --> 00:17:58.000
they come on board at certain points and-

00:17:58.000 --> 00:17:58.833
Yeah, right.

00:17:58.833 --> 00:18:00.933
It's always like, "What? You do this?"

00:18:00.933 --> 00:18:04.606
I mean, we've had fans
of our music for years

00:18:04.606 --> 00:18:07.403
who are like, "Yeah, what
about a serious album?"

00:18:07.403 --> 00:18:09.930
So, it kinda scratches that itch for them.

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:10.971
But there's a lot of people who,

00:18:10.971 --> 00:18:13.405
yeah, it's like they never
knew what sound it would be,

00:18:13.405 --> 00:18:14.930
that it would be so country.

00:18:14.930 --> 00:18:17.464
The most common response is,

00:18:17.464 --> 00:18:19.127
"I'm not a fan of country music,"

00:18:19.127 --> 00:18:22.973
or, "I hate country
music, but I love this."

00:18:22.973 --> 00:18:24.022
And it's interesting.

00:18:24.022 --> 00:18:25.297
I'm seeing the same exact thing

00:18:25.297 --> 00:18:26.602
'cause, you know, when
we're recording this

00:18:26.602 --> 00:18:30.493
I just put "Where We're
Going", the second single out,

00:18:30.493 --> 00:18:33.859
the same exact reaction in terms of,

00:18:33.859 --> 00:18:36.391
"I don't like country,
but I like this song too."

00:18:36.391 --> 00:18:37.615
And a lot of people are now saying,

00:18:37.615 --> 00:18:40.049
"Hey, it's not that you
don't like country music,

00:18:40.049 --> 00:18:42.855
it's that you've been listening
to the wrong country music.

00:18:42.855 --> 00:18:45.084
You've been listening
to radio country music.

00:18:45.084 --> 00:18:48.464
You've been listening to
what's popular on the radio,"

00:18:48.464 --> 00:18:49.576
nobody listens to the radio anymore,

00:18:49.576 --> 00:18:51.678
but you know what I'm saying,
it's a certain type of sound.

00:18:51.678 --> 00:18:52.622
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:18:52.622 --> 00:18:55.820
It's all these songs about
trucks and, you know,

00:18:55.820 --> 00:18:57.758
God and country or whatever.

00:18:57.758 --> 00:19:00.542
And it's, like, this is not that.

00:19:00.542 --> 00:19:01.899
And I think people, it's like,

00:19:01.899 --> 00:19:06.899
"Oh, you actually do like
broadly country folk, Americana,"

00:19:07.378 --> 00:19:09.475
however, there's multiple
ways to describe this.

00:19:09.475 --> 00:19:11.903
Yeah, you're turning people onto it.

00:19:11.903 --> 00:19:16.433
Well, I wanted to shout-out
a couple of moments.

00:19:16.433 --> 00:19:20.716
Well, the one lyric
early on, when you say,

00:19:20.716 --> 00:19:25.716
"'Cause the one I gave you
said that that ain't it,"

00:19:25.822 --> 00:19:28.787
I like that lyric because
you've got the you,

00:19:28.787 --> 00:19:30.223
which is the end of one sentence,

00:19:30.223 --> 00:19:32.928
the beginning in the next sentence, right?

00:19:32.928 --> 00:19:35.511
Am I hearing that right?
No-

00:19:35.511 --> 00:19:39.150
"'Cause the one I gave you
said that that ain't it".

00:19:39.150 --> 00:19:41.956
No, "'Cause the one I gave,
you said that ain't it".

00:19:41.956 --> 00:19:43.228
Oh. Oh.

00:19:43.228 --> 00:19:44.385
But you can hear it that way.

00:19:44.385 --> 00:19:47.644
I always hear it more colloquially.

00:19:47.644 --> 00:19:48.841
It also makes sense that way.

00:19:48.841 --> 00:19:51.472
I gave it to you, you said that ain't it.

00:19:51.472 --> 00:19:52.869
Well, I mean, that's
what I'm saying, yeah.

00:19:52.869 --> 00:19:53.770
It is what you're saying.

00:19:53.770 --> 00:19:56.597
But you actually didn't
do what I thought you did.

00:19:56.597 --> 00:19:59.906
No, I just said 'cause the one I gave,

00:19:59.906 --> 00:20:01.629
you said that ain't it.

00:20:01.629 --> 00:20:03.154
Yeah, that makes more sense.

00:20:03.154 --> 00:20:06.030
But it's also the one I gave
you, you said that ain't it.

00:20:06.030 --> 00:20:07.769
That needs to be how you,

00:20:07.769 --> 00:20:09.417
you need to take credit for that.

00:20:09.417 --> 00:20:10.287
Okay.

00:20:10.287 --> 00:20:11.809
Okay, I did it on purpose.

00:20:11.809 --> 00:20:14.892
What about, you know, in the chorus

00:20:16.508 --> 00:20:19.048
you say "My heart was never true,

00:20:19.048 --> 00:20:21.465
that may say more about you",

00:20:22.632 --> 00:20:26.825
Is that the type of thing
that it speaks for itself or?

00:20:26.825 --> 00:20:30.892
I'm curious, what does
this say about people?

00:20:30.892 --> 00:20:33.352
Well, I don't know.

00:20:33.352 --> 00:20:34.791
That's for them to figure out.

00:20:34.791 --> 00:20:36.021
Okay, yeah. I respect that.

00:20:36.021 --> 00:20:37.664
But I think that, you know,

00:20:37.664 --> 00:20:40.214
the reason that this is the first song

00:20:40.214 --> 00:20:42.802
is because it's, this is
what I explained before,

00:20:42.802 --> 00:20:44.410
musically, I think it kinda sets a tone.

00:20:44.410 --> 00:20:47.165
But, thematically, like the album order,

00:20:47.165 --> 00:20:49.344
more than the musical progression,

00:20:49.344 --> 00:20:52.544
I leaned into the thematic
progression of like,

00:20:52.544 --> 00:20:55.211
"If you were to sit down with me

00:20:56.079 --> 00:20:59.737
and I were to begin talking to
you about my deconstruction,"

00:20:59.737 --> 00:21:01.954
which is not even really
something that I want to do

00:21:01.954 --> 00:21:05.288
unless somebody starts asking me about it,

00:21:05.288 --> 00:21:08.121
especially now, I don't, you know,

00:21:09.575 --> 00:21:11.767
I'm not interested in
changing people's minds

00:21:11.767 --> 00:21:13.659
as much as I may have
been at an earlier point,

00:21:13.659 --> 00:21:18.659
but like, "What is the process
that I would lead you through

00:21:18.692 --> 00:21:20.834
as I was kind of trying
to explain myself," right?

00:21:20.834 --> 00:21:23.630
This is obviously a super
self-indulgent project

00:21:23.630 --> 00:21:25.036
on multiple levels.

00:21:25.036 --> 00:21:28.059
But also just the fact that I
think I got something to say

00:21:28.059 --> 00:21:29.758
and that you should listen to it, right?

00:21:29.758 --> 00:21:31.434
And there is self-indulgent.

00:21:31.434 --> 00:21:34.488
But if I am talking to you
regardless of where you're at

00:21:34.488 --> 00:21:36.373
on the spectrum of
spiritual deconstruction,

00:21:36.373 --> 00:21:41.290
Christianity, whatever, this
kinda sets the tone of saying,

00:21:42.325 --> 00:21:44.960
"The point of this is not for you to agree

00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:46.441
with what I'm saying.

00:21:46.441 --> 00:21:49.416
The point is just listen."

00:21:49.416 --> 00:21:50.479
If somebody is gonna tell you

00:21:50.479 --> 00:21:52.330
about their spiritual experience,

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:54.538
take their word that
they're the closest to it

00:21:54.538 --> 00:21:55.699
as they can possibly be

00:21:55.699 --> 00:21:57.335
and that they're being honest with you

00:21:57.335 --> 00:21:58.778
in what they're saying.

00:21:58.778 --> 00:22:02.479
And so if your perspective at
the end of the day is that,

00:22:02.479 --> 00:22:04.821
"Oh, you never were a Christian,"

00:22:04.821 --> 00:22:05.935
and you may be committed to that

00:22:05.935 --> 00:22:09.987
because of some theological
perspective that you have,

00:22:09.987 --> 00:22:11.596
again, that says more about you.

00:22:11.596 --> 00:22:15.385
In that instance, if you think
that I wasn't a Christian,

00:22:15.385 --> 00:22:17.372
actually there's nothing about me

00:22:17.372 --> 00:22:19.962
that demonstrates that
I wasn't a Christian;

00:22:19.962 --> 00:22:22.758
it's you and your commitment
to a specific ideology

00:22:22.758 --> 00:22:25.642
that makes you see me in a certain way.

00:22:25.642 --> 00:22:27.413
So, when you say my heart was never true,

00:22:27.413 --> 00:22:28.439
that says a lot more about you

00:22:28.439 --> 00:22:31.072
than it does about me in my experience.

00:22:31.072 --> 00:22:32.841
Yeah, and who wants to talk to somebody

00:22:32.841 --> 00:22:34.852
who's not gonna listen to,

00:22:34.852 --> 00:22:38.058
who's not going to believe
what they're saying,

00:22:38.058 --> 00:22:40.656
but instead just try to put 'em in a box

00:22:40.656 --> 00:22:43.573
and, like, mind their true motives.

00:22:44.654 --> 00:22:47.935
Like, assuming that you're lying to me,

00:22:47.935 --> 00:22:52.863
my goal in this conversation
is to find the real truth.

00:22:52.863 --> 00:22:54.754
That's what I'm gonna
do here today, Rhett.

00:22:54.754 --> 00:22:55.743
Just kidding.

00:22:55.743 --> 00:22:57.784
No, like, I know how that feels.

00:22:57.784 --> 00:23:00.790
Yeah, I mean, we felt a lot of that.

00:23:00.790 --> 00:23:05.316
And I think it's the
perfect start to the album.

00:23:05.316 --> 00:23:06.627
So, before I move to the next song,

00:23:06.627 --> 00:23:09.213
I wanna thank Casey Frank.

00:23:09.213 --> 00:23:10.604
You're gonna thank people every time, huh?

00:23:10.604 --> 00:23:13.145
Well, as we get new
instrument, instrumentalist.

00:23:13.145 --> 00:23:14.257
All right, fine.

00:23:14.257 --> 00:23:15.634
Casey Frank played that organ.

00:23:15.634 --> 00:23:16.726
That's a real organ.

00:23:16.726 --> 00:23:19.033
Like, we had a fake
organ in there originally

00:23:19.033 --> 00:23:23.700
and I was like, man, you know
me, I'm not a details guy.

00:23:25.596 --> 00:23:28.036
But Derek's like, "You know,
we gotta get a real organ,"

00:23:28.036 --> 00:23:28.869
and I'm glad he did.

00:23:28.869 --> 00:23:32.012
There's no fake instruments
in any of this stuff.

00:23:32.012 --> 00:23:34.888
That was recorded by Talley
Sherwood at Tritone Recording.

00:23:34.888 --> 00:23:39.888
And then the music video,
the lyric video/music video,

00:23:40.822 --> 00:23:43.905
again, that was done by Jax Anderson,

00:23:44.950 --> 00:23:46.738
she makes incredible music on her own

00:23:46.738 --> 00:23:48.039
and does incredible directing.

00:23:48.039 --> 00:23:50.614
And so shout-out to Jax Anderson.

00:23:50.614 --> 00:23:52.966
Buddy System Season One theme song.

00:23:52.966 --> 00:23:53.961
Yes.

00:23:53.961 --> 00:23:55.140
That's right.

00:23:55.140 --> 00:23:56.268
Yeah.

00:23:56.268 --> 00:23:59.092
Okay, for the second song,
this may be a surprise to you,

00:23:59.092 --> 00:24:00.984
this is actually responding to some input

00:24:00.984 --> 00:24:01.969
that I got from you,

00:24:01.969 --> 00:24:05.661
but also as I really
started thinking about what,

00:24:05.661 --> 00:24:07.685
there was a point at which
I thought I was gonna do

00:24:07.685 --> 00:24:09.127
the first three singles

00:24:09.127 --> 00:24:11.062
were gonna be the first
three songs on the album.

00:24:11.062 --> 00:24:13.361
But then as I started thinking,

00:24:13.361 --> 00:24:15.907
there's multiple things going into this.

00:24:15.907 --> 00:24:19.236
'Cause you're like, "Who am
I thinking about when I,"

00:24:19.236 --> 00:24:20.427
I'm thinking about the listener,

00:24:20.427 --> 00:24:22.227
I'm thinking about somebody who's like,

00:24:22.227 --> 00:24:23.098
"I like country music.

00:24:23.098 --> 00:24:24.495
Who's this new kid on the block,"

00:24:24.495 --> 00:24:27.161
or, "I'm a fan of Rhett and Link,

00:24:27.161 --> 00:24:28.571
and I don't even like country music,

00:24:28.571 --> 00:24:31.984
but what's Rhett got to say here?"

00:24:31.984 --> 00:24:34.493
I couldn't decide who I
was making that thing for,

00:24:34.493 --> 00:24:37.406
so I just stuck with themes

00:24:37.406 --> 00:24:38.585
and it just ended up being that

00:24:38.585 --> 00:24:40.991
we're still a very in,
like, classic country

00:24:40.991 --> 00:24:42.219
on the second song.

00:24:42.219 --> 00:24:45.981
I went with "Flash of
Rationality" as a second song.

00:24:45.981 --> 00:24:48.902
And we could talk about
it after we listen to it.

00:24:48.902 --> 00:24:49.863
All right I'm ready.

00:25:03.729 --> 00:25:08.729
♪ Sometimes I have a
flash of rationality ♪

00:25:10.918 --> 00:25:15.918
♪ But most times I do
just what I'm goin' do ♪

00:25:18.357 --> 00:25:23.357
♪ It's easy for me to
see what I already see ♪

00:25:25.427 --> 00:25:30.427
♪ Ain't got time for inconvenient truths ♪

00:25:33.416 --> 00:25:37.224
♪ I'm just a man, I ain't no machine ♪

00:25:37.224 --> 00:25:40.928
♪ I do the math the way it suits me ♪

00:25:40.928 --> 00:25:44.156
♪ Don't get me wrong,
you're making some sins ♪

00:25:44.156 --> 00:25:49.156
♪ But I think I'll go
before I am condemned ♪

00:25:51.219 --> 00:25:54.717
♪ Sometimes I start ♪

00:25:54.717 --> 00:25:55.868
I love that one.

00:25:55.868 --> 00:25:59.778
I mean, it goes even more country, like-

00:25:59.778 --> 00:26:00.807
It's probably the most country.

00:26:00.807 --> 00:26:01.831
Right down the middle.

00:26:01.831 --> 00:26:06.831
But, I mean, it's got this
very vintage country vibe.

00:26:07.038 --> 00:26:09.374
You know, it's got a
Charley Crockett type retro-

00:26:09.374 --> 00:26:11.063
Well, when you hear
something in your mind,

00:26:11.063 --> 00:26:13.548
it's one of those things
where, like, what I was,

00:26:13.548 --> 00:26:16.260
that's my homage to Merle, you know.

00:26:16.260 --> 00:26:17.656
That's why there's a trumpet in there.

00:26:17.656 --> 00:26:20.170
That's my homage to my favorite era,

00:26:20.170 --> 00:26:23.384
and your favorite era, of
Merle, late '70s, early '80s

00:26:23.384 --> 00:26:25.443
when he was, like, bringing in jazz.

00:26:25.443 --> 00:26:29.231
And, of course, once I put my spin on it,

00:26:29.231 --> 00:26:30.495
it ends up in a different place

00:26:30.495 --> 00:26:33.449
so you might have to be told
that that's the reference.

00:26:33.449 --> 00:26:36.360
But the reason I put that song there

00:26:36.360 --> 00:26:40.843
is, again, I wanna be like,
"Hey, this is a country album."

00:26:40.843 --> 00:26:42.166
It's the shortest song on the album,

00:26:42.166 --> 00:26:43.071
it's under three minutes,

00:26:43.071 --> 00:26:44.520
it's the only one that's
under three minutes,

00:26:44.520 --> 00:26:45.671
and so I kinda wanna be like,

00:26:45.671 --> 00:26:47.397
"All right, from streaming perspectives,

00:26:47.397 --> 00:26:50.808
like, I'm in at this album,
I get to that second song."

00:26:50.808 --> 00:26:52.996
But from a thematic
standpoint, it's kinda saying,

00:26:52.996 --> 00:26:56.247
"Listen, first song I told you,

00:26:56.247 --> 00:26:57.522
you don't have to agree with me.

00:26:57.522 --> 00:26:58.742
And in the second song, I'm telling you,

00:26:58.742 --> 00:27:00.707
I also don't know what I'm talking about."

00:27:00.707 --> 00:27:02.065
You know what I'm saying?

00:27:02.065 --> 00:27:03.286
Yeah, it's self-deprecating.

00:27:03.286 --> 00:27:06.995
I'm, like, I don't think I got
something incredible to say.

00:27:06.995 --> 00:27:09.703
I don't think I've got some
incredible insights into this.

00:27:09.703 --> 00:27:12.250
I think that I'm smarter
than I actually am.

00:27:12.250 --> 00:27:15.465
I buy a lot of books, I
don't finish reading them.

00:27:15.465 --> 00:27:17.309
Sometimes I think
straight about this stuff,

00:27:17.309 --> 00:27:18.701
a lot of times I don't.

00:27:18.701 --> 00:27:20.265
I'm just being honest with you.

00:27:20.265 --> 00:27:22.937
This isn't some treatise that
you're supposed to, like,

00:27:22.937 --> 00:27:25.397
be aligned with what's going on here.

00:27:25.397 --> 00:27:28.651
I'm just guessing it the same
way that you are, you know,

00:27:28.651 --> 00:27:30.987
it's kind of the thematic message

00:27:30.987 --> 00:27:32.994
and that's what I wanted
it to be at this point.

00:27:32.994 --> 00:27:34.306
And that trumpet.

00:27:34.306 --> 00:27:37.195
Yeah, I mean, it is a surprise
first time you hear it.

00:27:37.195 --> 00:27:40.239
Like, I mean, I heard it a
lot later in the album before.

00:27:40.239 --> 00:27:42.532
It was, like, maybe four
from the end when I heard it,

00:27:42.532 --> 00:27:45.115
and I was like, "Oh,
you gotta move this up."

00:27:45.115 --> 00:27:47.670
Especially when that
trumpet came in, it's like,

00:27:47.670 --> 00:27:50.878
"Oh, you give people this surprise."

00:27:50.878 --> 00:27:52.732
Well, I got a funny
story about the trumpet.

00:27:52.732 --> 00:27:55.576
So, I knew from the beginning
that I wanted a trumpet.

00:27:55.576 --> 00:27:57.595
And the way that I indicated
that I want a trumpet

00:27:57.595 --> 00:27:59.564
is when I sent Derek the demo,

00:27:59.564 --> 00:28:02.308
which is just me playing
a guitar and singing,

00:28:02.308 --> 00:28:03.825
it got to that part of the song

00:28:03.825 --> 00:28:05.794
and I just did a mouth trumpet.

00:28:05.794 --> 00:28:07.739
Yeah, as you would, I guess.

00:28:07.739 --> 00:28:10.368
And nothing like a good mouth trumpet.

00:28:10.368 --> 00:28:11.310
And he told me, he was like,

00:28:11.310 --> 00:28:13.453
"Hey, trumpet players, it's tough."

00:28:13.453 --> 00:28:16.411
He's like, "We can get guitarists all day,

00:28:16.411 --> 00:28:20.580
but trumpet, brass players,
they're harder to get,

00:28:20.580 --> 00:28:22.152
they're more expensive," whatever.

00:28:22.152 --> 00:28:23.096
And I was like, "Really?"

00:28:23.096 --> 00:28:25.599
Okay, and you knew you
wanted trumpet, not sax,

00:28:25.599 --> 00:28:30.205
because I mean, Merle he
traveled with a sax player,

00:28:30.205 --> 00:28:31.231
not a trumpet player.

00:28:31.231 --> 00:28:34.096
But he had some trumpet solos.

00:28:34.096 --> 00:28:37.118
And more like the Spanish, you know,

00:28:37.118 --> 00:28:38.806
influence songs actually.

00:28:38.806 --> 00:28:41.894
I was hearing trumpet more
than I was hearings sax,

00:28:41.894 --> 00:28:43.317
like, you know, like, muffled-like,

00:28:43.317 --> 00:28:44.589
but the thing on the end-

00:28:44.589 --> 00:28:47.042
Yeah, definitely makes sense.

00:28:47.042 --> 00:28:51.152
So then we actually talked
to, I talked to Ward,

00:28:51.152 --> 00:28:53.152
our friend Ward Roberts.

00:28:54.348 --> 00:28:56.091
You know, he's actually pretty
connected in the music world

00:28:56.091 --> 00:28:58.110
and his dad's like a jazz player.

00:28:58.110 --> 00:28:59.465
So I was like-
Trombone.

00:28:59.465 --> 00:29:02.436
"We can't find," and
he goes like, "I know,"

00:29:02.436 --> 00:29:04.195
and he sends me, like, a list.

00:29:04.195 --> 00:29:07.013
And Chris Bautista was the
first person that we contacted

00:29:07.013 --> 00:29:08.888
and he was down to do the project.

00:29:08.888 --> 00:29:11.245
And he was like, "What
do you want me to play?"

00:29:11.245 --> 00:29:15.523
And I said, "Well, I've
already done a mouth trumpet

00:29:15.523 --> 00:29:16.522
of the melody."

00:29:16.522 --> 00:29:17.835
And I was like, "You can play that,

00:29:17.835 --> 00:29:19.412
and then play some other stuff."

00:29:19.412 --> 00:29:22.425
And then at the end of the day,

00:29:22.425 --> 00:29:25.891
we just all liked just him
doing exactly what I did

00:29:25.891 --> 00:29:26.813
with the mouth trumpet.

00:29:26.813 --> 00:29:28.649
So, you got him to play a mouth trumpet.

00:29:29.614 --> 00:29:30.578
That sounded like a real trumpet to me.

00:29:30.578 --> 00:29:32.022
I'm gonna play you the demo.

00:29:32.022 --> 00:29:33.853
This is the only demo I'm gonna play

00:29:33.853 --> 00:29:35.377
because I want you to hear the final songs

00:29:35.377 --> 00:29:36.467
and I don't wanna be here forever.

00:29:36.467 --> 00:29:37.858
But I thought this was funny enough.

00:29:37.858 --> 00:29:39.608
You don't wanna be here forever?

00:29:39.608 --> 00:29:40.651
Where you gotta be?

00:29:40.651 --> 00:29:42.720
To play you the demo.

00:29:42.720 --> 00:29:44.163
The demo trumpet, all right.

00:29:44.163 --> 00:29:47.138
I'll play a little bit of
the song to see just how,

00:29:47.138 --> 00:29:49.896
what I do ain't as good
as what we do collectively

00:29:49.896 --> 00:29:52.816
once it gets in the hands of a producer.

00:29:52.816 --> 00:29:56.396
♪ I know just enough about enough stuff ♪

00:29:56.396 --> 00:29:59.512
♪ To not when you bring somethin' up ♪

00:29:59.512 --> 00:30:03.553
♪ Talking out of my ass is my MO ♪

00:30:03.553 --> 00:30:08.386
♪ I'm trying to learn
to say I don't know ♪

00:30:29.399 --> 00:30:30.711
Yeah, he did better than you, man.

00:30:31.931 --> 00:30:33.330
His mouth trumpet's a lot better.

00:30:33.330 --> 00:30:35.747
He made that sound awesome.

00:30:36.844 --> 00:30:40.339
And then he added the like .

00:30:40.339 --> 00:30:42.431
Yeah, yeah, a little ending.

00:30:42.431 --> 00:30:45.527
Yeah, so that's "Flash of Rationality".

00:30:45.527 --> 00:30:48.099
I mean, the melody was
intact, though, you know.

00:30:48.099 --> 00:30:49.625
He played the same thing.

00:30:49.625 --> 00:30:51.625
I'm talking about your singing melody.

00:30:51.625 --> 00:30:53.118
Oh yeah, that one,

00:30:53.118 --> 00:30:55.475
and this was the second to
last song written, by the way,

00:30:55.475 --> 00:30:57.725
the "Flash of Rationality".

00:30:58.697 --> 00:31:00.114
'Cause I wanted that theme of like,

00:31:00.114 --> 00:31:04.125
I started realizing that,
as you get into the album,

00:31:04.125 --> 00:31:07.187
I'm flirting with, the
whole thing is flirting

00:31:07.187 --> 00:31:09.938
with self-importance and
self, you know, indulgence.

00:31:09.938 --> 00:31:12.208
But I just wanted to remind you that,

00:31:12.208 --> 00:31:15.395
hey, I don't think I'm some smart-ass,

00:31:15.395 --> 00:31:18.242
I am a smart-ass, I'm more of
a smart-ass than a smart guy.

00:31:18.242 --> 00:31:21.002
So, just wanted to remind you.

00:31:21.002 --> 00:31:22.556
Yeah, I appreciate you

00:31:22.556 --> 00:31:25.531
moving it to a disclaimer
placement in your album

00:31:25.531 --> 00:31:26.619
instead of burying it.

00:31:26.619 --> 00:31:27.496
Yeah, right.

00:31:27.496 --> 00:31:28.762
And also the fact that

00:31:28.762 --> 00:31:31.294
it's one of my favorite
songs on the album.

00:31:31.294 --> 00:31:32.777
Yeah, it's a good vibe.

00:31:32.777 --> 00:31:36.431
You can walk down the street to it.

00:31:36.431 --> 00:31:39.101
You can walk back the other
way down the street to it.

00:31:39.101 --> 00:31:41.978
Third song is the third single

00:31:41.978 --> 00:31:45.684
that just came out a week
before the album dropped

00:31:45.684 --> 00:31:46.998
"Give a Damn".

00:31:46.998 --> 00:31:48.611
Okay, yes.

00:31:48.611 --> 00:31:51.055
So, I put this one
early in the mix as well

00:31:51.055 --> 00:31:53.808
and it's pretty big departure.

00:31:53.808 --> 00:31:54.729
Let's listen.

00:32:06.829 --> 00:32:10.374
♪ Ain't it funny how some guys who lived ♪

00:32:10.374 --> 00:32:12.843
♪ Well before your time ♪

00:32:12.843 --> 00:32:15.924
♪ Make you think what you think ♪

00:32:15.924 --> 00:32:18.745
♪ God thinks about wine ♪

00:32:18.745 --> 00:32:21.265
♪ The kind of thing will draw you in ♪

00:32:21.265 --> 00:32:24.210
♪ And you are like me ♪

00:32:24.210 --> 00:32:27.093
♪ Why live for the here and now ♪

00:32:27.093 --> 00:32:29.465
♪ When you've got eternity ♪

00:32:29.465 --> 00:32:32.778
♪ They say your life unexamined ♪

00:32:32.778 --> 00:32:35.550
♪ Is a life that's not worth living ♪

00:32:35.550 --> 00:32:38.700
♪ But I'm pretty sure I grayed my beard ♪

00:32:38.700 --> 00:32:43.700
♪ With all these fucks I've given ♪

00:32:44.387 --> 00:32:46.871
♪ I can't help but give a damn ♪

00:32:46.871 --> 00:32:50.355
♪ But damn, sometime I wish I didn't ♪

00:32:50.355 --> 00:32:53.758
♪ First time I heard of the great beyond ♪

00:32:53.758 --> 00:32:56.003
♪ I was surely smitten ♪

00:32:56.003 --> 00:32:59.440
♪ I could be one of the chosen few ♪

00:32:59.440 --> 00:33:01.956
♪ Be on the front lines ♪

00:33:01.956 --> 00:33:04.828
♪ I've got something in my pocket ♪

00:33:04.828 --> 00:33:08.995
♪ That'll give sight to the blind ♪

00:33:14.042 --> 00:33:16.668
Woo!

00:33:16.668 --> 00:33:19.014
Yeah, that's a good one, man.

00:33:19.014 --> 00:33:23.434
Yeah, I had already heard
"Believe Me" a lot early.

00:33:23.434 --> 00:33:25.536
I heard the first two singles-

00:33:25.536 --> 00:33:26.536
Really early-

00:33:26.536 --> 00:33:27.711
So by the time I listened to the album

00:33:27.711 --> 00:33:30.844
this is the one that was, like, let's see,

00:33:30.844 --> 00:33:33.104
we were on a plane.

00:33:33.104 --> 00:33:34.197
We were getting on a plane.

00:33:34.197 --> 00:33:36.120
You were listening to the album
and we were in the airport.

00:33:36.120 --> 00:33:37.775
Yeah, it was one of those things

00:33:37.775 --> 00:33:40.525
where I had to leave my phone off

00:33:43.716 --> 00:33:45.895
airport mode, airplane mode,

00:33:45.895 --> 00:33:47.503
even though we were taking off

00:33:47.503 --> 00:33:51.219
'cause I was trying to get
the last song finished.

00:33:51.219 --> 00:33:54.220
And I'm like, "Man, what's
gonna happen," all right?

00:33:54.220 --> 00:33:55.580
Somebody gonna come up to me

00:33:55.580 --> 00:33:57.460
and, like, kick me off this plane?

00:33:57.460 --> 00:33:59.944
Yeah, they threw you right out the door.

00:33:59.944 --> 00:34:01.531
You know, I had to get started to finish.

00:34:01.531 --> 00:34:04.856
But then my first, you
know, I leaned over to you.

00:34:04.856 --> 00:34:05.804
You happened to be there.

00:34:05.804 --> 00:34:08.598
It was such a coincidence.

00:34:08.598 --> 00:34:11.318
And I was like, "That
was the 'Give a Damn'."

00:34:11.318 --> 00:34:13.028
I was like, "Give a Damn".

00:34:13.028 --> 00:34:15.816
Whoa, that's the one right there.

00:34:15.816 --> 00:34:17.363
I mean, listening to it again here,

00:34:17.363 --> 00:34:18.430
I'll tell you what's my favorite,

00:34:18.430 --> 00:34:22.592
but that was, like, the first
one I wanted to talk about.

00:34:22.592 --> 00:34:23.832
So, let's talk about it.

00:34:23.832 --> 00:34:26.677
I mean, the guitar hook right off the bat.

00:34:29.958 --> 00:34:31.486
Did you do mouth guitar for that?

00:34:31.486 --> 00:34:32.319
Nope.

00:34:35.460 --> 00:34:37.690
My guess is that was part of the solo

00:34:37.690 --> 00:34:42.690
and then you retroactively moved
the second half of the solo

00:34:43.768 --> 00:34:47.066
to be the intro, but am I
getting too in the weeds here?

00:34:47.066 --> 00:34:50.650
Well, actually I don't know exactly,

00:34:50.650 --> 00:34:53.524
I don't know what that
process was exactly.

00:34:53.524 --> 00:34:57.264
Because, you know, obviously
this is the one demo

00:34:57.264 --> 00:35:00.058
that when I sent it over I added,

00:35:00.058 --> 00:35:02.481
you know, I record and logic,

00:35:02.481 --> 00:35:04.858
and then Derek is the pro-tools guys,

00:35:04.858 --> 00:35:07.868
but I did a beat, I was
like, "This is a rock song."

00:35:07.868 --> 00:35:12.868
This is like, in my mind, like
an Avett Brothers rock song

00:35:13.424 --> 00:35:15.402
'cause you've got that kind of like,

00:35:15.402 --> 00:35:19.177
no, but they don't do lead
guitar in the same way,

00:35:19.177 --> 00:35:20.904
but that's what I was hearing.

00:35:20.904 --> 00:35:21.892
Usually.

00:35:21.892 --> 00:35:22.725
And so-

00:35:23.656 --> 00:35:25.216
It's got that banjo in it, okay.

00:35:25.216 --> 00:35:27.512
So, I dropped a beat in there really

00:35:27.512 --> 00:35:29.822
so I could play with the groove
and it gives me the energy

00:35:29.822 --> 00:35:30.849
that I need to sing in the demo.

00:35:30.849 --> 00:35:32.872
It's the only song that I did that on.

00:35:32.872 --> 00:35:34.765
And I was just like, "Listen, I want,"

00:35:34.765 --> 00:35:36.086
there's a couple songs where I was like,

00:35:36.086 --> 00:35:38.351
"I want Alex to let loose on this

00:35:38.351 --> 00:35:42.882
because we need something
powerful and hooky on this."

00:35:42.882 --> 00:35:46.006
And, you know, typically
Derek was semi something

00:35:46.006 --> 00:35:48.277
and give me a couple options
to this that he sent over,

00:35:48.277 --> 00:35:49.389
and I was like, "That's it.

00:35:49.389 --> 00:35:51.442
That's that hook that it needed."

00:35:51.442 --> 00:35:52.307
Oh yeah.

00:35:52.307 --> 00:35:54.868
I mean, it's interesting
that you said it was rock

00:35:54.868 --> 00:35:57.133
because it did not turn out that way.

00:35:57.133 --> 00:36:00.633
I mean, it's quite a jaunt to use the word

00:36:01.774 --> 00:36:04.461
that you used in the first song, but-

00:36:04.461 --> 00:36:05.820
Folk rock.

00:36:05.820 --> 00:36:06.879
Yeah, okay.

00:36:06.879 --> 00:36:08.929
The Avett Brothers, if
you, like, go to the Wiki,

00:36:08.929 --> 00:36:10.321
it doesn't say they're a country band,

00:36:10.321 --> 00:36:11.554
it doesn't say they're a bluegrass band,

00:36:11.554 --> 00:36:14.992
it says they're a folk
rock band, you know.

00:36:14.992 --> 00:36:16.220
And I think this is a folk rock song.

00:36:16.220 --> 00:36:19.720
Like, the chicken pecking of that intro,

00:36:22.285 --> 00:36:25.202
actually that was very Merle to me.

00:36:26.256 --> 00:36:28.834
But I'm talking more '60s, which I liked.

00:36:28.834 --> 00:36:32.065
I don't know if you even felt
like that was a connection.

00:36:32.065 --> 00:36:34.370
But it was like "Workin' Man Blues".

00:36:34.370 --> 00:36:35.326
I hear that.

00:36:35.326 --> 00:36:38.409
I also hear a hint of Lynyrd Skynyrd.

00:36:40.132 --> 00:36:41.932
Yeah, the slide lead.
Slide guitar.

00:36:41.932 --> 00:36:43.088
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:36:43.088 --> 00:36:47.588
But I mean, you could hear
Jackson Browne in that too.

00:36:49.352 --> 00:36:50.424
I'm just saying it's interesting-

00:36:50.424 --> 00:36:52.052
I'll take a little Skynyrd.

00:36:52.052 --> 00:36:54.118
I mean, when you add that banjo

00:36:54.118 --> 00:36:57.443
and then you add a bunch of fucks given,

00:36:57.443 --> 00:36:58.892
This is the explicit lyrics song.

00:36:58.892 --> 00:37:02.476
Is it gonna have an E on-
Yeah.

00:37:02.476 --> 00:37:05.319
Oh yeah, you playing the
Mumford and Sons card.

00:37:05.319 --> 00:37:06.625
When I uploaded the song-

00:37:06.625 --> 00:37:08.375
There's an explicit track on this.

00:37:08.375 --> 00:37:09.669
Is this Mumford and Sons album?

00:37:09.669 --> 00:37:11.379
It doesn't mean as much as it used to.

00:37:11.379 --> 00:37:12.422
No, it doesn't.

00:37:12.422 --> 00:37:13.423
It doesn't mean as much as it used to.

00:37:13.423 --> 00:37:16.125
And also I've noticed
that, like, certain artists

00:37:16.125 --> 00:37:19.875
in this genre who, like,
if Father John Misty

00:37:20.750 --> 00:37:22.896
says the F word in a song,

00:37:22.896 --> 00:37:25.599
he doesn't put explicit lyrics on it.

00:37:25.599 --> 00:37:26.432
Interesting.

00:37:26.432 --> 00:37:28.232
It was like, "Well, I'm gonna do it.

00:37:28.232 --> 00:37:30.153
Okay, I'm gonna do it."

00:37:30.153 --> 00:37:33.510
One last thing about the
production, the gang vocals.

00:37:33.510 --> 00:37:37.399
Like, yeah, it just felt like it was,

00:37:37.399 --> 00:37:40.804
it was such a relief when that showed up.

00:37:40.804 --> 00:37:41.796
I just felt like-
A relief?

00:37:41.796 --> 00:37:43.075
Yeah, 'cause I was like,

00:37:43.075 --> 00:37:47.213
"Oh, that's where this
needed to go," you know.

00:37:47.213 --> 00:37:48.404
In a good way.

00:37:48.404 --> 00:37:49.642
Yeah, and that's just me and Derek

00:37:49.642 --> 00:37:53.373
standing in his studio
12 feet from the mic.

00:37:53.373 --> 00:37:56.003
Just me and you have done
gang vocals a hundred times,

00:37:56.003 --> 00:37:57.894
where you're trying to do
multiple voices and stuff.

00:37:57.894 --> 00:37:58.919
Did you do any Muppet voices?

00:37:58.919 --> 00:38:00.237
I didn't hear any Muppet voices.

00:38:00.237 --> 00:38:01.258
I said I'm not gonna do the high voice

00:38:01.258 --> 00:38:02.332
because that makes it
sound like the Muppets.

00:38:02.332 --> 00:38:03.615
I was like that's one
thing that I've learned

00:38:03.615 --> 00:38:04.521
from me and Link-

00:38:04.521 --> 00:38:07.972
Yeah, it was more, you
know, friends in low places-

00:38:07.972 --> 00:38:09.028
People at a bar.
Kind of a vibe.

00:38:09.028 --> 00:38:11.674
Kinda thing, yeah.
Yeah, bar sing-along.

00:38:11.674 --> 00:38:15.424
Yeah, definitely that
sealed the deal for me,

00:38:16.455 --> 00:38:18.549
between the guitar at the opening

00:38:18.549 --> 00:38:20.691
and then the gang vocals at the end.

00:38:20.691 --> 00:38:25.601
But this song, I mean, is
about evangelism, right?

00:38:25.601 --> 00:38:27.553
Yeah.

00:38:27.553 --> 00:38:30.357
But you can be an evangelist of anything.

00:38:30.357 --> 00:38:33.441
It's just that we're
very steeped in a church

00:38:33.441 --> 00:38:36.034
that had the label because it was such,

00:38:36.034 --> 00:38:37.680
so key to our-

00:38:37.680 --> 00:38:38.826
And as I talked about a lot of times,

00:38:38.826 --> 00:38:41.160
my personality is evangelistic.

00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:43.327
I like to persuade people,

00:38:44.456 --> 00:38:48.193
and I don't necessarily
like that about myself,

00:38:48.193 --> 00:38:51.487
and this is a song about, man,
I can't help but give a damn

00:38:51.487 --> 00:38:53.630
but sometimes I wish I didn't, you know.

00:38:53.630 --> 00:38:55.643
It doesn't make your life easier

00:38:55.643 --> 00:38:57.697
when you like to change people's minds.

00:38:57.697 --> 00:39:00.701
When you first heard
about the great beyond-

00:39:00.701 --> 00:39:02.054
And also yeah-
You were smitten.

00:39:02.054 --> 00:39:03.375
I think there's something about people

00:39:03.375 --> 00:39:06.596
who feel like there's
gotta be some purpose.

00:39:06.596 --> 00:39:08.364
They're naturally drawn to religion.

00:39:08.364 --> 00:39:10.384
And really when they're
involved in a religion,

00:39:10.384 --> 00:39:11.421
they really go for it.

00:39:11.421 --> 00:39:15.605
Like, I became a missionary
because I thought

00:39:15.605 --> 00:39:17.822
that this is the most
important thing in the world.

00:39:17.822 --> 00:39:19.142
Like, this is so much more important

00:39:19.142 --> 00:39:23.703
than every other earthly thing
that, "How do I go all in?"

00:39:23.703 --> 00:39:26.172
Well, you do it full-time.

00:39:26.172 --> 00:39:27.572
And so that's why I became a missionary.

00:39:27.572 --> 00:39:28.405
And so-

00:39:29.419 --> 00:39:30.610
Okay, go ahead.

00:39:30.610 --> 00:39:31.638
I have another question.

00:39:31.638 --> 00:39:33.647
And then so what?

00:39:33.647 --> 00:39:36.897
Yeah, but then when you switch teams,

00:39:39.194 --> 00:39:41.242
you become an evangelist for that team.

00:39:41.242 --> 00:39:43.638
Well, the second time you say it

00:39:43.638 --> 00:39:45.901
it's not the great beyond, it's-

00:39:45.901 --> 00:39:47.340
The first time I saw someone's eyes-

00:39:47.340 --> 00:39:48.430
Someone's eyes light up.

00:39:48.430 --> 00:39:53.347
So, is that, like, catching
the bug of converting somebody?

00:39:54.943 --> 00:39:55.859
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:39:55.859 --> 00:39:58.168
Like, if I can sit down and talk to you,

00:39:58.168 --> 00:40:01.350
like if you're a conservative Christian

00:40:01.350 --> 00:40:02.701
and I can have a conversation with you

00:40:02.701 --> 00:40:05.359
and make you question
some fundamental things

00:40:05.359 --> 00:40:09.200
and it seems like a light bulb goes off,

00:40:09.200 --> 00:40:10.509
there's something, you know,

00:40:10.509 --> 00:40:11.898
incredibly rewarding about that.

00:40:11.898 --> 00:40:14.511
It's the same part of
your brain that lights up

00:40:14.511 --> 00:40:17.777
when you're evangelizing
someone and they come to Christ.

00:40:17.777 --> 00:40:19.177
You think that you're doing it,

00:40:19.177 --> 00:40:21.584
and you might be doing it out of love

00:40:21.584 --> 00:40:25.123
and belief that this
person's soul is being saved,

00:40:25.123 --> 00:40:27.393
but it's incredibly, personally rewarding.

00:40:27.393 --> 00:40:30.104
If you were to scan somebody's
brain who was having,

00:40:30.104 --> 00:40:31.974
was doing evangelism and
someone was converting,

00:40:31.974 --> 00:40:35.473
the serotonin, you know, would be,

00:40:35.473 --> 00:40:37.964
whatever the brain chemical is,

00:40:37.964 --> 00:40:40.271
you would be getting that reward,

00:40:40.271 --> 00:40:44.072
you would be getting
the reward that you get

00:40:44.072 --> 00:40:46.604
from, you know, winning at a game.

00:40:46.604 --> 00:40:47.827
Dopamine, you know.

00:40:47.827 --> 00:40:50.916
It's a dopamine rush.
Okay.

00:40:50.916 --> 00:40:52.719
And that is in my personality-

00:40:52.719 --> 00:40:54.037
Hit the dopamine molecule.

00:40:54.037 --> 00:40:57.362
So, when I go to the other team, I,

00:40:57.362 --> 00:41:01.355
and, again, the music
video for this is out,

00:41:01.355 --> 00:41:02.388
the music video for this

00:41:02.388 --> 00:41:04.699
is currently not been finished being made.

00:41:04.699 --> 00:41:06.880
It's an animated music video from-

00:41:06.880 --> 00:41:07.912
You're telling the future.

00:41:07.912 --> 00:41:12.273
From Micah Buzan, who is
this incredible animator

00:41:12.273 --> 00:41:14.440
who made that music video.

00:41:15.731 --> 00:41:18.284
You know, the song is
a departure musically

00:41:18.284 --> 00:41:21.033
and so the visual is
very much a departure.

00:41:21.033 --> 00:41:24.170
And it's kinda symbolizing
this in sort of,

00:41:24.170 --> 00:41:27.095
as an analogy in a
different world kinda thing.

00:41:27.095 --> 00:41:29.905
But the whole point just being that,

00:41:29.905 --> 00:41:32.252
again, the reason this is the
third song is multipurpose.

00:41:32.252 --> 00:41:33.241
Number one, it's like,

00:41:33.241 --> 00:41:35.463
hey, this is another place
that we're going on this album.

00:41:35.463 --> 00:41:37.600
It's not just country.

00:41:37.600 --> 00:41:39.858
We're getting into some different stuff

00:41:39.858 --> 00:41:42.953
that is broadly country, but
this Americana, folk rock,

00:41:42.953 --> 00:41:44.758
whatever you wanna call it.

00:41:44.758 --> 00:41:46.037
But thematically, it's like,

00:41:46.037 --> 00:41:50.870
hey, I just told you that I'm
not, don't take me seriously

00:41:52.252 --> 00:41:53.559
'cause I'm not taking myself as seriously

00:41:53.559 --> 00:41:55.038
as I might seem sometimes,

00:41:55.038 --> 00:41:57.178
but also, hey, I don't even like the fact

00:41:57.178 --> 00:41:58.867
that I wanna change your mind.

00:41:58.867 --> 00:42:00.883
I don't even like that about myself.

00:42:00.883 --> 00:42:04.006
It's making me age more quickly.

00:42:04.006 --> 00:42:07.933
But, you know, I'm
processing it through music.

00:42:07.933 --> 00:42:11.749
The gray in your beard
started under the ears almost.

00:42:11.749 --> 00:42:13.065
And in the middle and.

00:42:13.065 --> 00:42:14.381
And now in the middle.

00:42:14.381 --> 00:42:16.757
Yeah, and that's, you know,

00:42:16.757 --> 00:42:17.907
there's multiple reasons for that.

00:42:17.907 --> 00:42:21.407
So this section of the album, three songs,

00:42:23.001 --> 00:42:26.409
where now that I've sort of thematically

00:42:26.409 --> 00:42:29.159
sort of tried to position
myself a little bit as like,

00:42:29.159 --> 00:42:30.381
this is how I want you to kinda take

00:42:30.381 --> 00:42:31.910
what's about to come out,

00:42:31.910 --> 00:42:33.482
before I start preaching at you

00:42:33.482 --> 00:42:34.750
because I am gonna preach at you

00:42:34.750 --> 00:42:36.407
before the end of the album,

00:42:36.407 --> 00:42:40.131
now I'm directing my process
towards people I love,

00:42:40.131 --> 00:42:42.961
starting with my parents,
then going to my wife,

00:42:42.961 --> 00:42:44.745
and then going to my kids.

00:42:44.745 --> 00:42:48.621
Because spiritual deconstruction,
which is an overused term,

00:42:48.621 --> 00:42:49.708
but you know what I'm talking about,

00:42:49.708 --> 00:42:52.330
basically moving away
from traditional beliefs

00:42:52.330 --> 00:42:54.467
or reorganizing the way
you think about this,

00:42:54.467 --> 00:42:58.183
reorienting yourself, and
in my case deconconverting,

00:42:58.183 --> 00:43:00.839
is very complicated for
the people that you love,

00:43:00.839 --> 00:43:05.032
especially if they are
still in that faith, right?

00:43:05.032 --> 00:43:08.132
And so this is a song that was written

00:43:08.132 --> 00:43:11.406
as I process the way that
I feel towards my parents

00:43:11.406 --> 00:43:13.580
and my parents feel towards me.

00:43:13.580 --> 00:43:14.886
It's called "Sorry".

00:43:32.997 --> 00:43:37.153
♪ I never set out to do ♪

00:43:37.153 --> 00:43:40.610
♪ Something to disappoint you ♪

00:43:40.610 --> 00:43:45.610
♪ Approval was sorta my thing ♪

00:43:49.612 --> 00:43:53.773
♪ Took your book and made it mine ♪

00:43:53.773 --> 00:43:57.930
♪ Never colored outside the lines ♪

00:43:57.930 --> 00:44:02.930
♪ And made my own peace with the king ♪

00:44:07.129 --> 00:44:10.988
♪ But then the integrity ♪

00:44:10.988 --> 00:44:15.039
♪ You'd poured deep inside of me ♪

00:44:15.039 --> 00:44:20.039
♪ Started to burn a hole in my heart ♪

00:44:23.909 --> 00:44:27.936
♪ I grabbed on that scarlet thread ♪

00:44:27.936 --> 00:44:31.691
♪ And pulled 'til there was nothing left ♪

00:44:31.691 --> 00:44:36.691
♪ And then my whole world fell apart ♪

00:44:41.007 --> 00:44:46.007
♪ You did nothing wrong ♪

00:44:49.130 --> 00:44:54.130
♪ And I did nothing wrong ♪

00:44:57.480 --> 00:45:02.480
♪ So why do I feel like I need ♪

00:45:03.083 --> 00:45:08.083
♪ To say sorry in a song ♪

00:45:11.801 --> 00:45:12.917
♪ I know you ♪

00:45:12.917 --> 00:45:14.005
What's the answer?

00:45:14.005 --> 00:45:18.844
Why do you feel like you
need to say sorry in a song?

00:45:18.844 --> 00:45:19.848
I don't know.

00:45:19.848 --> 00:45:21.552
I don't have the answer.

00:45:21.552 --> 00:45:24.052
I think this is something that

00:45:26.092 --> 00:45:29.462
anybody in this similar situation,

00:45:29.462 --> 00:45:32.154
like you love and respect your parents,

00:45:32.154 --> 00:45:33.821
you were a good kid,

00:45:36.817 --> 00:45:39.047
you know that this is a
disappointment to them,

00:45:39.047 --> 00:45:42.403
in spite of how well your life has gone

00:45:42.403 --> 00:45:44.751
or how well things are with your family

00:45:44.751 --> 00:45:47.014
and your career and all this stuff,

00:45:47.014 --> 00:45:50.155
if you disagree on this fundamental thing,

00:45:50.155 --> 00:45:51.596
it's a disappointment.

00:45:51.596 --> 00:45:53.259
You know, I think about my kids

00:45:53.259 --> 00:45:55.225
and I try to explain this to Locke

00:45:55.225 --> 00:45:58.715
to kinda get him to
understand the dynamic here.

00:45:58.715 --> 00:46:02.223
Even though my parents
really, really love me,

00:46:02.223 --> 00:46:06.556
because I have deconverted
and publicly deconverted,

00:46:07.766 --> 00:46:09.545
it's kind of a double whammy.

00:46:09.545 --> 00:46:11.339
Not only am I no longer a Christian,

00:46:11.339 --> 00:46:15.172
but I'm this public
figure who talks about it.

00:46:16.451 --> 00:46:18.590
You know the world that we come from

00:46:18.590 --> 00:46:21.388
and you know how those
kids are seen, right?

00:46:21.388 --> 00:46:26.274
You know how people see
me, and that impacts them.

00:46:26.274 --> 00:46:29.597
And the thing that I'm trying
to say in this song as well

00:46:29.597 --> 00:46:33.563
is that like, well, to
finish that thought,

00:46:33.563 --> 00:46:35.046
I was like, Locke, if, you know,

00:46:35.046 --> 00:46:37.394
we've kinda raised you according
to a certain worldview,

00:46:37.394 --> 00:46:40.192
it's a much more permeable worldview

00:46:40.192 --> 00:46:42.947
than the one that I was given.

00:46:42.947 --> 00:46:44.389
I'm not telling you exactly what to think,

00:46:44.389 --> 00:46:45.867
but there are certain values

00:46:45.867 --> 00:46:49.400
and there are certain things
that we hold, you know,

00:46:49.400 --> 00:46:53.587
leading with love and
being kinda the main value.

00:46:53.587 --> 00:46:54.941
And I was like, if you
were to, like, go off

00:46:54.941 --> 00:46:59.191
and become some guy who
is, like, contrary to this,

00:47:01.096 --> 00:47:02.929
you know, in some way,

00:47:04.086 --> 00:47:05.412
there's multiple ways
that you could do that,

00:47:05.412 --> 00:47:07.251
like, that would be difficult for me.

00:47:07.251 --> 00:47:09.443
But, multiply that times 10

00:47:09.443 --> 00:47:14.026
when I would become worried
about your eternal destiny,

00:47:15.514 --> 00:47:17.137
that's what my parents live with,

00:47:17.137 --> 00:47:19.116
and that's what the parents of many people

00:47:19.116 --> 00:47:22.345
who've deconstructed and
deconverted live with.

00:47:22.345 --> 00:47:24.490
Thankfully, we still
have a great relationship

00:47:24.490 --> 00:47:26.324
and we love each other.

00:47:26.324 --> 00:47:30.350
And love trumps everything else, right?

00:47:30.350 --> 00:47:33.306
The love is stronger than the faith,

00:47:33.306 --> 00:47:35.227
which that's what Jesus
himself said, right?

00:47:35.227 --> 00:47:37.053
Faith, hope, and love, the
greatest of these is love.

00:47:37.053 --> 00:47:40.636
If you lose the love,
you've lost the plot.

00:47:42.960 --> 00:47:44.210
But I feel bad.

00:47:45.146 --> 00:47:48.479
Like, the idea of my mom crying about me

00:47:51.100 --> 00:47:54.059
in my eternal destiny, that hurts.

00:47:54.059 --> 00:47:57.559
Yeah, it hurts you because it hurts her.

00:47:59.210 --> 00:48:02.637
You know, you broke their hearts.

00:48:02.637 --> 00:48:04.729
And you didn't do anything wrong,

00:48:04.729 --> 00:48:07.562
but you certainly don't, you know,

00:48:08.531 --> 00:48:10.851
the sign-up sheet to
break your parents' hearts

00:48:10.851 --> 00:48:15.014
is not something that people
are lining up for, you know,

00:48:15.014 --> 00:48:19.265
especially with the type of
relationship that you have,

00:48:19.265 --> 00:48:22.959
you not only had, but have with them.

00:48:22.959 --> 00:48:26.047
Yeah, so you're able to
say, "You did nothing wrong-

00:48:26.047 --> 00:48:27.032
And what I mean by that-

00:48:27.032 --> 00:48:30.357
But it's hard for you to say, you know,

00:48:30.357 --> 00:48:31.703
they've done nothing wrong either.

00:48:31.703 --> 00:48:34.434
The reason I say they've done
nothing wrong is because,

00:48:34.434 --> 00:48:38.302
again, I know what it's like
to be an evangelical Christian.

00:48:38.302 --> 00:48:41.885
And when your kids
depart, not only do you,

00:48:42.860 --> 00:48:44.794
like, it's built into that community

00:48:44.794 --> 00:48:47.345
that you become responsible for it.

00:48:47.345 --> 00:48:50.507
The Bible basically implies
that in a couple of places

00:48:50.507 --> 00:48:52.390
that, like, your kids' outcome,

00:48:52.390 --> 00:48:54.240
I mean, it's a gray area for sure,

00:48:54.240 --> 00:48:55.762
there's ways to interpret
and get around it.

00:48:55.762 --> 00:48:58.798
But, like, regardless
of what the Bible says,

00:48:58.798 --> 00:49:01.839
the evangelical community blames parents

00:49:01.839 --> 00:49:03.700
for what their kids end up doing.

00:49:03.700 --> 00:49:05.867
And so that's the feeling.

00:49:07.687 --> 00:49:09.255
It's like, "You didn't do anything wrong,"

00:49:09.255 --> 00:49:10.946
like, when I talk about the fact that,

00:49:10.946 --> 00:49:12.512
that's why I did that Instagram post like,

00:49:12.512 --> 00:49:14.081
"You guys taught me the gospel.

00:49:14.081 --> 00:49:15.558
I understand what the gospel means.

00:49:15.558 --> 00:49:17.484
I understand grace by faith.

00:49:17.484 --> 00:49:18.960
I'm not legalistic.

00:49:18.960 --> 00:49:20.807
You guys were never legalistic,"

00:49:20.807 --> 00:49:23.637
I didn't miss the boat in that way.

00:49:23.637 --> 00:49:24.624
I mean, yeah, it kinda-

00:49:24.624 --> 00:49:27.629
You didn't do anything wrong.

00:49:27.629 --> 00:49:30.652
Parents get bad rap across
the board, you know.

00:49:30.652 --> 00:49:33.662
It's like, "Why is so and
so guilty of this crime

00:49:33.662 --> 00:49:35.065
or now in prison?"

00:49:35.065 --> 00:49:40.045
It's like, "Well, how did their
parents fail 'em," you know.

00:49:40.045 --> 00:49:41.115
And I'll go a step further and say

00:49:41.115 --> 00:49:43.249
not only did they not do anything wrong,

00:49:43.249 --> 00:49:45.060
they did a lot of things right.

00:49:45.060 --> 00:49:46.406
And this is a very difficult thing

00:49:46.406 --> 00:49:48.873
that I'm saying in this song that I can't,

00:49:48.873 --> 00:49:49.775
you know, I haven't-

00:49:49.775 --> 00:49:51.919
The integrity that they gave you

00:49:51.919 --> 00:49:52.931
burned a hole in your heart.

00:49:52.931 --> 00:49:56.235
My parents' concern with truth,

00:49:56.235 --> 00:49:59.160
especially my dad because
he thinks a lot like me.

00:49:59.160 --> 00:50:01.948
My dad became a Christian
in his early 30s,

00:50:01.948 --> 00:50:04.166
so a later life conversion, right?

00:50:04.166 --> 00:50:06.331
So, it was a very strong conversion

00:50:06.331 --> 00:50:08.059
that was immediately, like, impacted him

00:50:08.059 --> 00:50:08.960
in so many different ways.

00:50:08.960 --> 00:50:09.821
And my mom as well.

00:50:09.821 --> 00:50:13.399
They both became Christians
at the same time.

00:50:13.399 --> 00:50:16.539
In his concern, his
lifelong concern with truth

00:50:16.539 --> 00:50:18.057
and putting truth above everything else,

00:50:18.057 --> 00:50:19.741
my personality's exactly the same way.

00:50:19.741 --> 00:50:22.322
And the thing that I want
to be able to say is that,

00:50:22.322 --> 00:50:25.117
"Hey, the same thing that got you

00:50:25.117 --> 00:50:26.930
to suddenly wake up in your early 30s

00:50:26.930 --> 00:50:28.656
and realize that life has got to be

00:50:28.656 --> 00:50:32.156
about more than just
going through the motions,

00:50:32.156 --> 00:50:34.088
like there's gotta be more to this life,

00:50:34.088 --> 00:50:38.130
and that led you to making a
profession of faith in Christ,"

00:50:38.130 --> 00:50:42.081
that was truly life-changing, I believe.

00:50:42.081 --> 00:50:44.222
That same instinct is what brought me

00:50:44.222 --> 00:50:47.093
to the conclusions that I came to.

00:50:47.093 --> 00:50:49.929
So, it's actually what
you did right, ironically,

00:50:49.929 --> 00:50:52.726
to make me really concerned
about truth and like,

00:50:52.726 --> 00:50:55.151
"Hey, if this is the most important thing,

00:50:55.151 --> 00:50:57.738
I wanna understand, is it really true?"

00:50:57.738 --> 00:51:01.605
And I came to my conclusion
that I am at right now,

00:51:01.605 --> 00:51:03.250
I always leave room, I say right now

00:51:03.250 --> 00:51:05.674
because I leave a lot of things open,

00:51:05.674 --> 00:51:07.317
is that it's not true, fundamentally.

00:51:07.317 --> 00:51:08.150
It's not true.

00:51:08.150 --> 00:51:09.505
Christianity is not true.

00:51:09.505 --> 00:51:11.106
If I thought that it was true,

00:51:11.106 --> 00:51:12.960
I would still be a part of it.

00:51:12.960 --> 00:51:14.193
I don't think that it's true.

00:51:14.193 --> 00:51:15.465
I think that there's a
lot of things about it

00:51:15.465 --> 00:51:17.132
that you can't know.

00:51:19.528 --> 00:51:20.547
But what I'm saying is like,

00:51:20.547 --> 00:51:23.310
even though I made a
lot of these decisions

00:51:23.310 --> 00:51:25.971
because of the good things
that you passed along,

00:51:25.971 --> 00:51:28.640
I know it still deeply disappoints you

00:51:28.640 --> 00:51:30.736
and I'm sorry about that.

00:51:30.736 --> 00:51:32.653
So, the part of like,

00:51:34.267 --> 00:51:37.850
why do I feel like I
need to say I'm sorry,

00:51:38.905 --> 00:51:42.905
like, yeah, I think that,
I know that resonates,

00:51:43.867 --> 00:51:45.865
that totally makes sense.

00:51:45.865 --> 00:51:48.228
But let's talk about the in a song part.

00:51:48.228 --> 00:51:52.579
Why do I feel like I need
to say I'm sorry in a song?

00:51:52.579 --> 00:51:54.840
You know, are there certain things

00:51:54.840 --> 00:51:58.086
that you're saying in this song

00:51:58.086 --> 00:52:01.086
that you couldn't say to their face?

00:52:02.982 --> 00:52:04.809
I think that applies to this,

00:52:04.809 --> 00:52:07.865
this song and many other ones.

00:52:07.865 --> 00:52:11.018
I don't talk this way to
people directly, you know,

00:52:11.018 --> 00:52:12.742
even in the next couple
songs we'll listen to.

00:52:12.742 --> 00:52:13.726
It's the same thing.

00:52:13.726 --> 00:52:15.614
It's like, I can say it
a lot better in a song

00:52:15.614 --> 00:52:17.916
than I can just sitting
down and saying it to you.

00:52:17.916 --> 00:52:18.898
Specifically with my parents,

00:52:18.898 --> 00:52:22.887
it's complicated by the
fact that it's so emotional

00:52:22.887 --> 00:52:26.587
and I can't have a real
conversation about this, you know,

00:52:26.587 --> 00:52:28.521
without, especially my mom.

00:52:28.521 --> 00:52:29.760
Like, she's heartbroken, right?

00:52:29.760 --> 00:52:34.186
So, we just don't talk
about this stuff directly.

00:52:34.186 --> 00:52:37.474
We talk about the things that, you know,

00:52:37.474 --> 00:52:39.698
moms and sons talk about, about life

00:52:39.698 --> 00:52:41.639
and what are the kids
doing and that kinda thing.

00:52:41.639 --> 00:52:42.583
And maybe we will get

00:52:42.583 --> 00:52:44.107
where we can talk more
directly about this stuff,

00:52:44.107 --> 00:52:46.524
but it's too hurtful.
Yeah.

00:52:47.415 --> 00:52:50.748
I mean, you saying the song that, like,

00:52:51.762 --> 00:52:55.368
we're probably not gonna change
each other's minds, right?

00:52:55.368 --> 00:52:56.682
Yeah.
So then you're left

00:52:56.682 --> 00:53:00.849
with just kind of, it
becomes this emotional thing

00:53:04.599 --> 00:53:09.035
that, you know, it is
still may be really raw.

00:53:09.035 --> 00:53:10.118
Yeah, yeah.

00:53:11.187 --> 00:53:14.020
So, did you feel afraid to even,

00:53:18.469 --> 00:53:20.636
to put the song out there?

00:53:22.797 --> 00:53:24.898
Are there hopes attached to it

00:53:24.898 --> 00:53:27.288
in terms of how you can process
it in that relationship?

00:53:27.288 --> 00:53:30.871
Or is that none of the
listener's business?

00:53:33.583 --> 00:53:34.613
Yeah, I would say that.

00:53:34.613 --> 00:53:36.422
I don't know. I don't know, you know.

00:53:36.422 --> 00:53:40.785
I think a lot of these songs
are just as much for, you know,

00:53:40.785 --> 00:53:42.332
the reason I didn't just write this song

00:53:42.332 --> 00:53:44.507
and sent it to my parents
and I made it a public thing

00:53:44.507 --> 00:53:48.507
is because I think this
will resonate, you know.

00:53:51.158 --> 00:53:52.275
This is happening.

00:53:52.275 --> 00:53:56.133
This is a worldwide cultural movement

00:53:56.133 --> 00:54:00.367
of people moving out of
these types of belief systems

00:54:00.367 --> 00:54:04.852
that their parents hold very
sacred and it's difficult.

00:54:04.852 --> 00:54:07.150
And there's no solution
in this, other than like,

00:54:07.150 --> 00:54:08.764
"Hey, I'm just identifying
with the emotion

00:54:08.764 --> 00:54:11.014
that this stirs in people."

00:54:12.176 --> 00:54:15.728
I think it's absolutely beautiful song

00:54:15.728 --> 00:54:18.061
in the way that it resonates

00:54:19.039 --> 00:54:21.789
even beyond the context of, like,

00:54:23.443 --> 00:54:26.814
changing your religious beliefs, you know.

00:54:26.814 --> 00:54:30.135
And I think we've talked
about this before, you know.

00:54:30.135 --> 00:54:33.979
Your relationship with your
parent, parents, guardians,

00:54:33.979 --> 00:54:35.689
like the people who, you know,

00:54:35.689 --> 00:54:36.778
people who are older than you,

00:54:36.778 --> 00:54:39.046
as you grow up and you
become an adult yourself,

00:54:39.046 --> 00:54:43.129
it's like the nature of
our relationships change.

00:54:44.197 --> 00:54:49.197
And sometimes if they don't,
that's weirder than if they do.

00:54:49.390 --> 00:54:53.240
But the changes that you go
through in that, you know,

00:54:53.240 --> 00:54:58.037
people who love you and were
there for you your whole life

00:54:58.037 --> 00:54:59.715
they're making different decisions,

00:54:59.715 --> 00:55:03.079
they're landing in a different place,

00:55:03.079 --> 00:55:07.493
it's a scary thing to
navigate a relationship

00:55:07.493 --> 00:55:10.950
where you have a fundamental
disagreement, you know.

00:55:10.950 --> 00:55:14.181
You talk about issues
of identity, you know,

00:55:14.181 --> 00:55:17.578
that whether it could still be religious,

00:55:17.578 --> 00:55:21.247
it could be, you know, relational, sexual,

00:55:21.247 --> 00:55:22.733
whatever the case may be

00:55:22.733 --> 00:55:26.091
there's so much that you have to navigate.

00:55:26.091 --> 00:55:28.008
I think it's your story

00:55:28.989 --> 00:55:30.797
and I think that it will resonate

00:55:30.797 --> 00:55:34.652
with other people's stories
in a way that it just kinda,

00:55:34.652 --> 00:55:35.988
it sprouts wings.

00:55:35.988 --> 00:55:38.556
And that's why I think
this is a beautiful song.

00:55:38.556 --> 00:55:43.556
Even if you're just doing
the spiritual exploration,

00:55:43.679 --> 00:55:45.564
I think it's a great album.

00:55:45.564 --> 00:55:49.647
But this is a standout
because it's so relatable,

00:55:50.706 --> 00:55:52.459
sadly, to so many people.

00:55:52.459 --> 00:55:55.126
And the irony is, is that, yeah,

00:55:57.428 --> 00:55:59.114
you don't have a lot of the baggage,

00:55:59.114 --> 00:56:01.499
like, from your parents.

00:56:01.499 --> 00:56:03.606
I mean, we all have
baggage, but, like, you-

00:56:03.606 --> 00:56:04.998
But it's a lot worse for a lot of people.

00:56:04.998 --> 00:56:09.366
You have an active relationship
where, like, you know,

00:56:09.366 --> 00:56:12.743
you still wanna spend
time together at holidays

00:56:12.743 --> 00:56:14.963
and, like, you care about
each other and it's not,

00:56:14.963 --> 00:56:17.553
a wall has not been built up.

00:56:17.553 --> 00:56:18.998
You still have a relationship.

00:56:18.998 --> 00:56:19.831
Yeah.

00:56:20.666 --> 00:56:25.556
You know, listen, I give them
a lot of credit for that.

00:56:25.556 --> 00:56:29.360
Because in the system that
they're in, it would not be,

00:56:29.360 --> 00:56:31.126
there are certain biblical passages

00:56:31.126 --> 00:56:34.172
that you could decide
you were gonna follow

00:56:34.172 --> 00:56:36.554
that would mean you would
cut off a relationship,

00:56:36.554 --> 00:56:39.485
just to be honest with you.

00:56:39.485 --> 00:56:41.744
And they're choosing love.

00:56:41.744 --> 00:56:46.161
Yeah, but that doesn't mean
that this wasn't deeply

00:56:50.617 --> 00:56:54.367
emotional and difficult
for you and for them.

00:56:56.383 --> 00:56:59.845
It's like kind of, you know,
it's under a best case scenario

00:56:59.845 --> 00:57:03.081
that it happened and you're
still illustrating the depth

00:57:03.081 --> 00:57:07.866
with which, like, there's
parts that you can only express

00:57:07.866 --> 00:57:09.555
in certain ways and, like,

00:57:09.555 --> 00:57:13.137
you're not able to get into it still.

00:57:13.137 --> 00:57:15.986
It's like, I just think about the people

00:57:15.986 --> 00:57:20.986
who is, like, everything else,
you know, to add onto it.

00:57:21.049 --> 00:57:24.492
So, I think it's a big one, man.

00:57:24.492 --> 00:57:26.363
And I wanted to put it
there 'cause, you know,

00:57:26.363 --> 00:57:29.153
that's one of my favorite
songs on the album.

00:57:29.153 --> 00:57:31.115
I just love the way that one came together

00:57:31.115 --> 00:57:33.601
and to kinda start that section.

00:57:33.601 --> 00:57:35.653
Of course, the next song
is the second single,

00:57:35.653 --> 00:57:38.754
which is me writing a song to my wife.

00:57:38.754 --> 00:57:40.713
It's called "Where We're Going".

00:57:54.404 --> 00:57:58.240
♪ Picked up on it first thing ♪

00:57:58.240 --> 00:58:01.575
♪ You weren't like the ones I'd known ♪

00:58:01.575 --> 00:58:04.922
♪ Might have been from the same place ♪

00:58:04.922 --> 00:58:08.408
♪ Coming from somewhere all your own ♪

00:58:08.408 --> 00:58:11.895
♪ A few hours on a wooden bench ♪

00:58:11.895 --> 00:58:15.336
♪ That's all it took to do me in ♪

00:58:15.336 --> 00:58:18.684
♪ Knew then I had to make it quick ♪

00:58:18.684 --> 00:58:22.470
♪ Before I made us fall into sin ♪

00:58:22.470 --> 00:58:27.470
♪ I don't know where we're going ♪

00:58:27.900 --> 00:58:32.900
♪ But I know I want you to go with me ♪

00:58:36.041 --> 00:58:41.041
♪ Don't care much about the destination ♪

00:58:41.415 --> 00:58:46.415
♪ As long as you're there
that's where I wanna be ♪

00:58:49.264 --> 00:58:52.902
♪ Talking took a different tone ♪

00:58:52.902 --> 00:58:54.694
♪ Started to ♪

00:58:54.694 --> 00:58:59.027
You know, it's amazing for
a song that is so sweet

00:59:00.997 --> 00:59:05.580
to be about going through so
much difficulty, you know?

00:59:08.686 --> 00:59:10.533
I mean, especially when you throw that,

00:59:10.533 --> 00:59:12.366
like, classic country,

00:59:14.676 --> 00:59:16.072
and I don't mean in terms of time,

00:59:16.072 --> 00:59:18.992
I mean just the way that, you know,

00:59:18.992 --> 00:59:21.125
the sweetness of a sweet country song,

00:59:21.125 --> 00:59:25.117
especially when you go
to that bridge at the end

00:59:25.117 --> 00:59:27.378
or whatever you call it, where it's like,

00:59:27.378 --> 00:59:29.063
"Now I'm gonna put this
all in perspective."

00:59:29.063 --> 00:59:30.955
It's like you followed this template,

00:59:30.955 --> 00:59:32.481
which is like, "I'm telling you a story

00:59:32.481 --> 00:59:33.751
about a relationship.

00:59:33.751 --> 00:59:35.891
You can see the music video in your head."

00:59:35.891 --> 00:59:39.226
Typically, the country music
singer would be singing-

00:59:39.226 --> 00:59:40.297
And you'd see the couple.

00:59:40.297 --> 00:59:42.509
And you see the couple
and it wouldn't be them,

00:59:42.509 --> 00:59:44.166
but you did the right thing.

00:59:44.166 --> 00:59:47.006
You put yourself and Jessie in the video.

00:59:47.006 --> 00:59:47.993
Right, yeah.

00:59:47.993 --> 00:59:52.585
Yeah, that was, if you didn't
know, that was Jessie singing.

00:59:52.585 --> 00:59:55.092
Yeah, and Ben Eck here,

00:59:55.092 --> 00:59:59.509
who's been at Mythical for
almost a decade, you know,

01:00:00.478 --> 01:00:03.336
I wanted the whole thing
to be outside of Mythical,

01:00:03.336 --> 01:00:07.067
but he was so excited when
he heard about the project.

01:00:07.067 --> 01:00:08.809
He was like, "Hey, whatever
I could do to help.

01:00:08.809 --> 01:00:10.555
I just wanna be a part of it."

01:00:10.555 --> 01:00:12.384
And so we collaborated on the video

01:00:12.384 --> 01:00:14.251
and he brought it to life.

01:00:14.251 --> 01:00:16.240
But, yeah, getting Jessie to sing,

01:00:16.240 --> 01:00:21.088
again, I wrote this song for
her for a Valentine's Day

01:00:21.088 --> 01:00:22.921
two years ago or 2021,

01:00:24.300 --> 01:00:28.300
and that was back before
this album was a thing,

01:00:30.902 --> 01:00:35.902
it was before I even knew that
I was making an album really,

01:00:36.312 --> 01:00:39.350
or that it was a country album, right?

01:00:39.350 --> 01:00:41.966
So, this song wasn't even
conceptualized as a country song

01:00:41.966 --> 01:00:44.210
the first time I wrote it.

01:00:44.210 --> 01:00:45.956
You couldn't help yourself, though.

01:00:45.956 --> 01:00:47.069
Yeah, I remember.

01:00:47.069 --> 01:00:49.573
Yeah, you talked about
singing this song to her

01:00:49.573 --> 01:00:50.910
on the podcast.

01:00:50.910 --> 01:00:52.577
You alluded to that.

01:00:53.529 --> 01:00:55.776
This is the first song
you wrote for the album.

01:00:55.776 --> 01:00:56.847
You didn't even know it.

01:00:56.847 --> 01:00:59.345
Well, it's not the first song I wrote

01:00:59.345 --> 01:01:00.347
'cause I was just writing songs-

01:01:00.347 --> 01:01:01.312
What's the first?
As a process.

01:01:01.312 --> 01:01:05.084
The first song I wrote is
the last song on the album.

01:01:05.084 --> 01:01:09.308
This may be the second song that I wrote.

01:01:09.308 --> 01:01:11.466
But, again, there was
a number of songs there

01:01:11.466 --> 01:01:12.707
in the middle that were,

01:01:12.707 --> 01:01:13.829
I didn't know I was making an album.

01:01:13.829 --> 01:01:17.436
I'm just writing music
and, like, recording demos.

01:01:17.436 --> 01:01:20.897
I mean, we've talked about it a lot

01:01:20.897 --> 01:01:24.028
when we talk about the deconstruction,

01:01:24.028 --> 01:01:25.484
in the deconstruction episodes.

01:01:25.484 --> 01:01:29.379
But I mean, it brings it into sharp focus

01:01:29.379 --> 01:01:33.701
how when your relationship
is built so strongly

01:01:33.701 --> 01:01:36.118
on the rock of Christ, right,

01:01:37.454 --> 01:01:40.121
that whenever you decide to move

01:01:41.274 --> 01:01:45.941
your home base somewhere else,
that is a scary prospect.

01:01:47.176 --> 01:01:49.997
And if you both don't
make that move together,

01:01:49.997 --> 01:01:53.602
and, again, we didn't make the move

01:01:53.602 --> 01:01:55.852
at the same time, you know.

01:01:56.849 --> 01:01:59.102
I kinda moved first.

01:01:59.102 --> 01:02:01.812
And then Jesse was, you
know, obviously scared,

01:02:01.812 --> 01:02:03.607
resistant, crying, that's
what I talk about in the song,

01:02:03.607 --> 01:02:05.475
it's just like when I
started having these like,

01:02:05.475 --> 01:02:07.261
"This is what I'm thinking.

01:02:07.261 --> 01:02:08.631
I really didn't wanna tell you about this

01:02:08.631 --> 01:02:10.291
'cause I know how much it'll upset you,"

01:02:10.291 --> 01:02:12.615
she would just cry,
you know, it was tough.

01:02:12.615 --> 01:02:13.448
It's one of the reasons

01:02:13.448 --> 01:02:16.576
it was such a long and lengthy process.

01:02:16.576 --> 01:02:18.576
But we eventually got to

01:02:19.527 --> 01:02:22.864
where we're not in exactly
the same place spiritually,

01:02:22.864 --> 01:02:24.934
it isn't like she wants me to be anywhere

01:02:24.934 --> 01:02:26.474
or I want her to be anywhere,

01:02:26.474 --> 01:02:31.307
we both are like we're untethered
from orthodoxy, frankly.

01:02:35.856 --> 01:02:39.384
But there's so many couples
where that is not the case.

01:02:39.384 --> 01:02:41.706
You're in very different
places fundamentally.

01:02:41.706 --> 01:02:44.203
And not only is that just
intrinsically difficult,

01:02:44.203 --> 01:02:45.824
but you've been told,

01:02:45.824 --> 01:02:49.567
if you're from a conservative
evangelical background,

01:02:49.567 --> 01:02:53.270
that you cannot be unequally
yoked, that it doesn't work.

01:02:53.270 --> 01:02:56.506
And you're also told that
everything that you have in life,

01:02:56.506 --> 01:03:00.016
every good instinct that
you have comes from God,

01:03:00.016 --> 01:03:01.132
comes from the spirit of God;

01:03:01.132 --> 01:03:04.000
and everything bad comes from you.

01:03:04.000 --> 01:03:07.170
Again, I'm not speaking
for all your evangelicals,

01:03:07.170 --> 01:03:10.507
but this is sort of the
perspective that I had.

01:03:10.507 --> 01:03:11.717
All your good instincts are from God,

01:03:11.717 --> 01:03:13.880
all your bad instincts are from you.

01:03:13.880 --> 01:03:15.419
And what that means is that the moment

01:03:15.419 --> 01:03:17.295
that you begin thinking
that this God thing

01:03:17.295 --> 01:03:21.037
may not make sense in the same way,

01:03:21.037 --> 01:03:22.562
you're just naturally programmed to think

01:03:22.562 --> 01:03:25.481
that only bad is now left.

01:03:25.481 --> 01:03:28.441
And now what are we gonna
base this marriage on?

01:03:28.441 --> 01:03:30.818
Love?

01:03:30.818 --> 01:03:32.566
I thought we had to base it on Jesus.

01:03:32.566 --> 01:03:36.133
So, this is an example
of some of the things

01:03:36.133 --> 01:03:39.960
we were warned about turned out
to be pretty good, you know.

01:03:39.960 --> 01:03:41.580
There was a lot of trepidation.

01:03:41.580 --> 01:03:43.871
Like, I definitely relate
with me and Christy

01:03:43.871 --> 01:03:45.324
in our spiritual journeys.

01:03:45.324 --> 01:03:47.436
Like, the trepidation of everything

01:03:47.436 --> 01:03:50.304
that you realize you
believed, if it wasn't,

01:03:50.304 --> 01:03:52.134
I mean, it was overtly
taught in a lot of ways,

01:03:52.134 --> 01:03:55.051
like you're talking about
the unequally yoked thing,

01:03:55.051 --> 01:03:58.051
but just how it permeates everything

01:04:00.154 --> 01:04:02.821
you don't even know you believe.

01:04:05.017 --> 01:04:09.190
You can feel it as fear before
you can state it logically.

01:04:09.190 --> 01:04:11.726
And when you're in a
deconstruction process, it's like,

01:04:11.726 --> 01:04:15.809
yeah, you're in this state
of fear a lot of times

01:04:17.195 --> 01:04:19.528
and you can't articulate it.

01:04:20.510 --> 01:04:22.049
But to get through that

01:04:22.049 --> 01:04:25.374
with anything that you
get through as a couple

01:04:25.374 --> 01:04:29.023
and if you do come out
stronger on the other side,

01:04:29.023 --> 01:04:31.273
then you can say, "Oh, wow,

01:04:33.316 --> 01:04:35.141
maybe it was worth it," you know, right?

01:04:35.141 --> 01:04:36.887
Can't you say that?

01:04:36.887 --> 01:04:40.804
I think that when you
strip away the ideology

01:04:43.435 --> 01:04:45.226
that was holding you together

01:04:45.226 --> 01:04:50.059
and it really becomes about
just love and this commitment,

01:04:53.650 --> 01:04:56.817
and, again, I know I give
a lot of these disclaimers,

01:04:56.817 --> 01:05:00.716
I'm not saying that
people who are Christians,

01:05:00.716 --> 01:05:03.711
who are in a relationship,
that it is all about,

01:05:03.711 --> 01:05:07.666
like, it isn't about love,
it's just about their ideology.

01:05:07.666 --> 01:05:10.792
The thing I would say, that I
would challenge Christians on

01:05:10.792 --> 01:05:13.256
is I actually think that, you know,

01:05:13.256 --> 01:05:17.342
a lot of guys in Christian
circles, this is what you hear.

01:05:17.342 --> 01:05:19.716
They're like, "Man, if
I wasn't a Christian,

01:05:19.716 --> 01:05:21.971
I'd be cheating on my wife."

01:05:21.971 --> 01:05:24.931
This is a very common sentiment.

01:05:24.931 --> 01:05:29.931
And I'm like, "Dude, that's
some up fucked up shit, man."

01:05:30.147 --> 01:05:33.191
Like, "Think about what you just said.

01:05:33.191 --> 01:05:35.568
Think about what you just said.

01:05:35.568 --> 01:05:37.570
What if the reason you
don't cheat on your wife

01:05:37.570 --> 01:05:39.653
is because you love her?"

01:05:43.666 --> 01:05:45.081
And here's what I would say,

01:05:45.081 --> 01:05:48.707
"I think the reason,"
'cause here's the deal,

01:05:48.707 --> 01:05:51.360
infidelity happens a whole lot

01:05:51.360 --> 01:05:53.685
with inside and outside of the church.

01:05:53.685 --> 01:05:55.228
It seems like that commitment

01:05:55.228 --> 01:05:57.056
actually doesn't play much of a role.

01:05:57.056 --> 01:05:58.342
And so if you are faithful,

01:05:58.342 --> 01:06:01.093
I think it actually means
that you're faithful.

01:06:01.093 --> 01:06:02.755
You don't have to constantly give credit

01:06:02.755 --> 01:06:05.456
to some external source for
every good thing you do.

01:06:05.456 --> 01:06:06.871
Maybe you're faithful

01:06:06.871 --> 01:06:11.538
because you have the ability
to be faithful in yourself.

01:06:13.690 --> 01:06:15.859
So that is an example of like,

01:06:15.859 --> 01:06:17.480
"Oh, this relationship that is,"

01:06:17.480 --> 01:06:20.705
and, listen, I'm not perfect,
Jessie's not perfect,

01:06:20.705 --> 01:06:23.777
I'm not saying that it's
always gonna be great,

01:06:23.777 --> 01:06:26.941
it has continually gotten
better over the years for us

01:06:26.941 --> 01:06:29.067
as we've grown together,

01:06:29.067 --> 01:06:32.969
but who knows what challenges
we'll meet, you know?

01:06:32.969 --> 01:06:36.210
But when the rubber meets the road,

01:06:36.210 --> 01:06:37.950
I want our relationship to be based

01:06:37.950 --> 01:06:39.646
on mutual love and respect

01:06:39.646 --> 01:06:42.259
and a commitment that is independent

01:06:42.259 --> 01:06:45.592
of our worldview and ideology, you know.

01:06:47.390 --> 01:06:48.890
And I think that that's actually the case

01:06:48.890 --> 01:06:49.977
in most relationships,

01:06:49.977 --> 01:06:52.732
even the ones where the
ideology is present.

01:06:52.732 --> 01:06:57.261
What do you mean by "More
than a savior we needed help"?

01:06:57.261 --> 01:06:59.880
'Cause, you know, I think from the,

01:06:59.880 --> 01:07:04.486
I don't know, that's
more of a head scratcher.

01:07:04.486 --> 01:07:06.569
What I meant by that is

01:07:08.388 --> 01:07:12.388
in the evangelical circles
that we ran around in

01:07:14.833 --> 01:07:19.304
you get a lot of help from a
spiritual perspective, right?

01:07:19.304 --> 01:07:20.548
And even when you go to therapy

01:07:20.548 --> 01:07:22.371
or you go to couple's counseling,

01:07:22.371 --> 01:07:27.371
it's always run through this
very specific biblical grid.

01:07:27.813 --> 01:07:30.223
And it's just like, "If your
relationship with Christ

01:07:30.223 --> 01:07:32.602
is intact and flourishing,

01:07:32.602 --> 01:07:35.395
then everything else in
your life will be okay,"

01:07:35.395 --> 01:07:38.179
is kind of the implication, right?

01:07:38.179 --> 01:07:42.441
And I think there are sometimes
when you just need help,

01:07:42.441 --> 01:07:46.751
you just actually need
somebody to help you

01:07:46.751 --> 01:07:48.053
navigate your relationship,

01:07:48.053 --> 01:07:52.903
navigate your own emotional and
mental well-being, you know.

01:07:52.903 --> 01:07:55.213
It's funny 'cause I know Christians

01:07:55.213 --> 01:08:00.213
who prefer, for their
therapists, to not be a Christian

01:08:00.387 --> 01:08:03.351
because they don't want it run
through this biblical grid,

01:08:03.351 --> 01:08:06.663
which a lot of times is
more hurtful than helpful.

01:08:06.663 --> 01:08:08.443
So, I think that the
point there is that like,

01:08:08.443 --> 01:08:11.940
"Hey, it isn't just about getting saved

01:08:11.940 --> 01:08:15.153
and having this Savior," this
saving knowledge of Christ,

01:08:15.153 --> 01:08:17.986
it's like, no, like couples need help.

01:08:17.986 --> 01:08:20.935
They just don't need to be right with God.

01:08:20.935 --> 01:08:22.592
They need to figure this .

01:08:22.592 --> 01:08:26.987
They need to figure out how
to navigate a relationship.

01:08:26.987 --> 01:08:28.510
And I think what we needed,

01:08:28.510 --> 01:08:32.324
each individually in our own circumstances

01:08:32.324 --> 01:08:35.722
and situations, but also as a couple,

01:08:35.722 --> 01:08:37.915
like we needed to work on us

01:08:37.915 --> 01:08:41.254
apart from just getting our
worldview straightened out.

01:08:41.254 --> 01:08:43.589
Like, that's just not the case.

01:08:43.589 --> 01:08:45.174
It's just not true

01:08:45.174 --> 01:08:48.714
that if you get the
right belief, you know,

01:08:48.714 --> 01:08:50.484
so much of conservative
evangelical Christianity

01:08:50.484 --> 01:08:51.974
is about believing the right things.

01:08:51.974 --> 01:08:54.516
You go to church on Sunday,
and what do they talk about?

01:08:54.516 --> 01:08:56.627
Well, hold on now, we're
not to the preachy-

01:08:56.627 --> 01:08:57.703
I can't help myself.
Preachy songs yet.

01:08:57.703 --> 01:09:00.091
We're getting there.

01:09:00.091 --> 01:09:01.435
And that's our experience, is that,

01:09:01.435 --> 01:09:02.782
hey, when we actually started thinking

01:09:02.782 --> 01:09:05.568
about our relationship and
thinking about each other

01:09:05.568 --> 01:09:08.485
rather than God, things got better.

01:09:10.235 --> 01:09:13.162
What's next?

01:09:13.162 --> 01:09:14.296
This is the third

01:09:14.296 --> 01:09:16.902
in this three-song
section to people I love,

01:09:16.902 --> 01:09:20.504
and this is the song
that I wrote for my boys.

01:09:20.504 --> 01:09:22.004
Okay, buckle up.

01:09:23.846 --> 01:09:25.301
It's called "Creek and Back".

01:09:53.442 --> 01:09:58.442
♪ Held you up before God and man ♪

01:10:01.501 --> 01:10:06.501
♪ Swore to raise you according to a plan ♪

01:10:08.025 --> 01:10:12.492
♪ Then the blueprint ♪

01:10:12.492 --> 01:10:17.492
♪ Faded to gray ♪

01:10:20.812 --> 01:10:25.812
♪ Still a lot that I want for you ♪

01:10:28.920 --> 01:10:33.920
♪ Still a lot that I think is true ♪

01:10:35.161 --> 01:10:40.127
♪ It's just now the path ain't ♪

01:10:40.127 --> 01:10:45.127
♪ Quite so straight ♪

01:10:47.646 --> 01:10:52.646
♪ The secret I probably should not tell ♪

01:10:52.945 --> 01:10:57.945
♪ Is that I still feel like a kid myself ♪

01:11:00.044 --> 01:11:05.044
♪ Been in over my head
since we brought you home ♪

01:11:05.841 --> 01:11:10.841
♪ Biggest thing that I've
learned is what I don't know ♪

01:11:12.225 --> 01:11:17.225
♪ But I'm your dad, I know that ♪

01:11:17.448 --> 01:11:22.031
♪ And I love you to the creek and back ♪

01:11:24.978 --> 01:11:27.811
Another tearjerker, come on now!

01:11:28.713 --> 01:11:32.666
So those three together, you
just want everybody to cry.

01:11:32.666 --> 01:11:34.779
Yeah.
Yeah, it's got wings, man.

01:11:34.779 --> 01:11:39.082
Like I said, in just like
I was talking about before,

01:11:39.082 --> 01:11:41.250
you know, any parent's gonna say,

01:11:41.250 --> 01:11:44.834
"Oh God, I know exactly
what that feels like,"

01:11:44.834 --> 01:11:48.167
to just, should I say that I'm clueless.

01:11:51.770 --> 01:11:53.293
I'm just as clueless as you,

01:11:53.293 --> 01:11:56.728
but in a different way, you know.

01:11:56.728 --> 01:12:00.847
It's extremely powerful
to put that out there.

01:12:00.847 --> 01:12:02.550
And I know it's gonna
resonate with people.

01:12:02.550 --> 01:12:05.046
And the piano doesn't hurt.

01:12:05.046 --> 01:12:09.104
Yeah, I wanted to, you
know, do a piano song.

01:12:09.104 --> 01:12:12.058
And it's not me playing the piano.

01:12:12.058 --> 01:12:13.226
I know that I learned

01:12:13.226 --> 01:12:16.132
how to play the piano for a tour we did,

01:12:16.132 --> 01:12:20.526
but that's a real piano
player, Edan Dover.

01:12:20.526 --> 01:12:24.604
And, of course, you know,
I wrote it on guitar,

01:12:24.604 --> 01:12:27.104
but I was hearing it as piano.

01:12:30.761 --> 01:12:31.804
And also it's kinda interesting

01:12:31.804 --> 01:12:34.905
because this is the kind
of music that, like,

01:12:34.905 --> 01:12:37.848
it's a little more, you
know, it's piano based

01:12:37.848 --> 01:12:39.841
so you're kinda getting into, you know,

01:12:39.841 --> 01:12:41.167
it's not like a Billy Joel

01:12:41.167 --> 01:12:44.622
or a, you know, Elton John song,

01:12:44.622 --> 01:12:47.322
but it's the most like that
of anything on the album,

01:12:47.322 --> 01:12:49.437
which is kinda like music
that we've connected,

01:12:49.437 --> 01:12:52.846
I've connected with the boys over.

01:12:52.846 --> 01:12:54.893
But yeah, exactly.

01:12:54.893 --> 01:12:56.681
I had written those
two songs to, you know,

01:12:56.681 --> 01:12:58.134
a song to my parents, song to my wife,

01:12:58.134 --> 01:13:00.010
and I was like, you know,

01:13:00.010 --> 01:13:05.010
I think that when you have a
fundamental shift in worldview,

01:13:05.321 --> 01:13:09.480
like, again, held you up
before God and man, the first,

01:13:09.480 --> 01:13:10.857
that's the dedication,

01:13:10.857 --> 01:13:13.983
which incidentally is a piece of video

01:13:13.983 --> 01:13:16.363
in the home video footage

01:13:16.363 --> 01:13:17.948
in the "Where We're Going" music video-

01:13:17.948 --> 01:13:18.990
Oh wow.

01:13:18.990 --> 01:13:21.731
Is meet us up there in front
of the church with Locke.

01:13:21.731 --> 01:13:22.564
Yeah.

01:13:25.299 --> 01:13:27.822
Which is a ceremony, arguably,

01:13:27.822 --> 01:13:29.949
just as much, if not,
more about the parents

01:13:29.949 --> 01:13:31.317
than it is about the kid.

01:13:31.317 --> 01:13:32.350
It's basically saying

01:13:32.350 --> 01:13:34.497
you get up in front of
everybody and you say,

01:13:34.497 --> 01:13:36.997
"I promise to raise this kid."

01:13:37.914 --> 01:13:39.239
You respond to a series of questions.

01:13:39.239 --> 01:13:44.239
I'm raising this kid, you
know, according to the gospel,

01:13:44.508 --> 01:13:47.953
and you raise them to serve
Jesus and to make a decision.

01:13:47.953 --> 01:13:49.034
Yeah.

01:13:49.034 --> 01:13:52.188
I mean, in my church, my
kids were actually baptized

01:13:52.188 --> 01:13:53.940
and there was an added thing of like-

01:13:53.940 --> 01:13:56.857
Well, that didn't save 'em, Link.

01:13:58.621 --> 01:14:00.790
You know, that clinging to this promise

01:14:00.790 --> 01:14:02.227
that's open to interpretation,

01:14:02.227 --> 01:14:04.825
that's like, okay, they are already saved

01:14:04.825 --> 01:14:07.825
as long as everybody does their job?

01:14:09.343 --> 01:14:13.775
Well, you're kinda claiming
them for Christ, you know.

01:14:13.775 --> 01:14:17.427
To me, this song is more powerful

01:14:17.427 --> 01:14:20.774
knowing at what stage of
fatherhood you wrote it.

01:14:20.774 --> 01:14:24.009
Because I think there's a lot of artists

01:14:24.009 --> 01:14:25.842
that will have a child

01:14:27.311 --> 01:14:28.879
and then of course they're
gonna start writing songs

01:14:28.879 --> 01:14:29.878
about their baby.

01:14:29.878 --> 01:14:31.966
There's so many songs,

01:14:31.966 --> 01:14:35.712
like an endless playlist of
this artist that you know

01:14:35.712 --> 01:14:36.882
had their first baby

01:14:36.882 --> 01:14:38.402
and then they had to
write a song about 'em.

01:14:38.402 --> 01:14:39.749
Sturgill's whole album,

01:14:39.749 --> 01:14:42.705
"A Sailor's Guide to the
Universe" or whatever it is,

01:14:42.705 --> 01:14:44.486
it's like it's all based
on him having a kid.

01:14:44.486 --> 01:14:47.044
I mean, Childish Gambino.

01:14:47.044 --> 01:14:51.567
I mean, but you're looking
at this baby and you're like,

01:14:51.567 --> 01:14:54.441
"Ever since the day I brought you home,

01:14:54.441 --> 01:14:58.004
I've been scared shitless," you know.

01:14:58.004 --> 01:14:59.399
So for you to say that

01:14:59.399 --> 01:15:02.649
and, you know, Locke is off at college.

01:15:03.538 --> 01:15:05.141
Thank God.

01:15:05.141 --> 01:15:07.792
And Shepherd's not as far behind

01:15:07.792 --> 01:15:09.315
as it seems like, you know, so it's--

01:15:09.315 --> 01:15:11.182
Meaning that it happened.

01:15:11.182 --> 01:15:12.567
I don't mean I'm thankful he's gone,

01:15:12.567 --> 01:15:14.790
I'm saying that, like, he got there.

01:15:14.790 --> 01:15:17.701
So, you see my point, right?

01:15:17.701 --> 01:15:22.618
I mean, it's strangely comforting
as a parent to hear this,

01:15:24.575 --> 01:15:26.908
knowing that you've got kids

01:15:28.613 --> 01:15:31.446
that are on the cusp of adulthood.

01:15:31.446 --> 01:15:33.403
Well, I think it goes back
to what we've talked about

01:15:33.403 --> 01:15:35.609
as we talk about relating to our kids

01:15:35.609 --> 01:15:39.137
that it's much less about
the things that you say

01:15:39.137 --> 01:15:41.314
and much more about
the things that you do.

01:15:41.314 --> 01:15:42.481
And, you know,

01:15:45.031 --> 01:15:48.781
I think modeling
cluelessness to my children,

01:15:51.604 --> 01:15:52.645
that's a harsh way to put it,

01:15:52.645 --> 01:15:55.330
but modeling humility or whatever

01:15:55.330 --> 01:15:57.421
or however you wanna say it like,

01:15:57.421 --> 01:16:00.630
"Hey, man, everything that we've done

01:16:00.630 --> 01:16:03.241
has been motivated by love.

01:16:03.241 --> 01:16:05.754
But it's all guesswork,

01:16:05.754 --> 01:16:09.053
and it's the best guess
that we have at the time.

01:16:09.053 --> 01:16:12.617
And I know for a fact that
you're going to be processing

01:16:12.617 --> 01:16:15.700
the mistakes that I've made forever."

01:16:17.735 --> 01:16:19.304
Do you know what I mean?

01:16:19.304 --> 01:16:20.137
Yeah.

01:16:22.846 --> 01:16:24.579
I mean, the song is for them first, right?

01:16:24.579 --> 01:16:28.579
Hopefully, just hearing
that is helpful to them.

01:16:29.901 --> 01:16:31.204
I'm glad-
So you don't end up

01:16:31.204 --> 01:16:32.851
with a bunch of resentment, you know.

01:16:32.851 --> 01:16:37.851
A parent who can't admit that
they made a bunch of mistakes,

01:16:38.296 --> 01:16:40.406
that is much more trauma-inducing

01:16:40.406 --> 01:16:42.739
than the mistakes sometimes.

01:16:44.194 --> 01:16:46.635
Yeah. I mean, it definitely piles it on.

01:16:46.635 --> 01:16:50.552
I'm glad that you didn't
go, like, cute country

01:16:52.305 --> 01:16:57.121
and then change "I love you
to the creek and back" too

01:16:57.121 --> 01:16:59.098
in the final chorus saying,

01:16:59.098 --> 01:17:02.628
"I love you with my creaking back."

01:17:02.628 --> 01:17:03.754
Oh, I get it.

01:17:03.754 --> 01:17:05.183
Like, I'm so old.
Got it.

01:17:05.183 --> 01:17:07.887
"My back sounds like a door."

01:17:07.887 --> 01:17:08.886
That would've been a bad-

01:17:08.886 --> 01:17:10.235
It's not too late.
Mistake.

01:17:10.235 --> 01:17:11.887
It's not too late.

01:17:11.887 --> 01:17:13.757
It is too late. Where are we going?

01:17:13.757 --> 01:17:16.055
Well, "Where We're Going"

01:17:16.055 --> 01:17:19.533
is that was the song I wrote to Jessie.

01:17:19.533 --> 01:17:21.068
You said "Where We're Going".

01:17:21.068 --> 01:17:23.230
From the song title-
What song is next?

01:17:23.230 --> 01:17:25.921
Well, before we move on, I wanna say,

01:17:25.921 --> 01:17:27.988
so obviously that was Jessie singing along

01:17:27.988 --> 01:17:29.737
with that song as well.

01:17:29.737 --> 01:17:31.200
So Jessie sings on three songs.

01:17:31.200 --> 01:17:32.988
She's the mother of those children.

01:17:32.988 --> 01:17:36.155
And so she did an amazing job there.

01:17:38.916 --> 01:17:41.815
We wanted to the cello and the violin,

01:17:41.815 --> 01:17:43.436
the strings were, like,
a last minute addition

01:17:43.436 --> 01:17:45.514
because we had just an
electric bass in there

01:17:45.514 --> 01:17:48.127
that Derek had played.

01:17:48.127 --> 01:17:50.195
You know, I was like, "I'm
hearing something else,"

01:17:50.195 --> 01:17:51.532
and then I heard a song,

01:17:51.532 --> 01:17:53.736
I was listening to some song
and there was piano and cello

01:17:53.736 --> 01:17:55.853
and I was like, "That's what we need."

01:17:55.853 --> 01:17:57.519
So we got Dave Eggar to play cello

01:17:57.519 --> 01:17:59.627
and Lisa Fako to play violin.

01:17:59.627 --> 01:18:02.655
So thank you for that,
making that song what it is.

01:18:02.655 --> 01:18:05.774
Now we're getting back into,

01:18:05.774 --> 01:18:08.338
this song is called "Only Thing",

01:18:08.338 --> 01:18:12.088
and this is, again, sort
of back to the processing

01:18:12.088 --> 01:18:13.822
'cause I'm about to move into a place

01:18:13.822 --> 01:18:16.561
where I'm not really
singing two individuals,

01:18:16.561 --> 01:18:18.335
but I'm more kinda in my own head

01:18:18.335 --> 01:18:23.335
and then finally directing
some things out to the church.

01:18:23.472 --> 01:18:24.383
Got a couple of songs

01:18:24.383 --> 01:18:26.511
that are directed at the
church towards the end.

01:18:26.511 --> 01:18:29.118
But this is back to, like, processing.

01:18:29.118 --> 01:18:31.080
Okay.

01:18:31.080 --> 01:18:32.119
"Only Thing".

01:18:47.241 --> 01:18:50.820
♪ I could argue with the best of them ♪

01:18:50.820 --> 01:18:55.433
♪ About where we came from ♪

01:18:55.433 --> 01:19:00.433
♪ Convince you over coffee
that Jesus was God's Son ♪

01:19:03.259 --> 01:19:08.259
♪ Take that empty feeling
that everybody feels ♪

01:19:11.652 --> 01:19:16.652
♪ And use it against you
'till you were on your heels ♪

01:19:18.545 --> 01:19:22.359
♪ I was sure as sure can be ♪

01:19:22.359 --> 01:19:27.330
♪ I was working for eternity ♪

01:19:27.330 --> 01:19:30.301
♪ There was hell to pay ♪

01:19:30.301 --> 01:19:33.889
♪ If you saw it another way ♪

01:19:33.889 --> 01:19:37.326
♪ Now the only thing I'm certain of ♪

01:19:37.326 --> 01:19:41.993
♪ Is that I ain't certain about much ♪

01:19:41.993 --> 01:19:46.993
♪ It's easy to be right it's
not so easy to choose love ♪

01:19:49.717 --> 01:19:54.717
♪ Sometimes I miss that feeling
of having it figured out ♪

01:19:57.960 --> 01:20:00.991
♪ Have to fight that temptation ♪

01:20:00.991 --> 01:20:04.991
♪ To know what life's all about ♪

01:20:16.567 --> 01:20:21.430
So sometimes it's, I don't
think it's sometimes,

01:20:21.430 --> 01:20:24.013
you said it's easy to be right,

01:20:26.584 --> 01:20:29.211
you said sometimes it's
not easy to choose love.

01:20:29.211 --> 01:20:31.337
So you're talking about this-

01:20:31.337 --> 01:20:34.174
It's easy to be right, it's
not so easy to choose love.

01:20:34.174 --> 01:20:37.674
So it's like that's the struggle, right?

01:20:41.281 --> 01:20:45.031
I mean, you're speaking
from experience here.

01:20:46.563 --> 01:20:47.396
Yeah.

01:20:50.348 --> 01:20:52.681
This one is just kinda that,

01:20:53.958 --> 01:20:58.708
I think that it's very difficult
when you're a Christian.

01:21:02.541 --> 01:21:03.960
And I would say, when you adhere

01:21:03.960 --> 01:21:06.564
to any particular religious philosophy

01:21:06.564 --> 01:21:07.599
and you really adhere to it,

01:21:07.599 --> 01:21:10.586
like you're pretty sure that
you're right about, right?

01:21:10.586 --> 01:21:14.586
Like, being right about
it is kind of important.

01:21:15.957 --> 01:21:17.874
There's a lot at stake.

01:21:20.694 --> 01:21:23.277
Specifically, I mean, you know,

01:21:25.101 --> 01:21:28.598
believing the creed, believing
the right thing about Jesus

01:21:28.598 --> 01:21:30.747
is the ticket to heaven, right?

01:21:30.747 --> 01:21:33.286
I mean, ultimately when you break it down,

01:21:33.286 --> 01:21:35.317
you gotta think and believe the right,

01:21:35.317 --> 01:21:38.737
you gotta believe the right thing.

01:21:38.737 --> 01:21:42.154
And your eternal destiny depends on that.

01:21:43.576 --> 01:21:47.358
And I think that is difficult
when you're in that situation,

01:21:47.358 --> 01:21:49.022
to envision what it would be like

01:21:49.022 --> 01:21:51.024
to just not be sure about that.

01:21:51.024 --> 01:21:53.441
That's one of the scariest things for me

01:21:53.441 --> 01:21:56.839
when I was transitioning
away from Christianity,

01:21:56.839 --> 01:22:00.135
which is like, "Whoa,"
like, "This is so crazy,"

01:22:00.135 --> 01:22:02.512
to, like, just accept the fact

01:22:02.512 --> 01:22:05.223
that you don't have to know
what you think about this stuff,

01:22:05.223 --> 01:22:08.973
that you could be
uncertain about this stuff.

01:22:10.260 --> 01:22:11.254
And it's really hard.

01:22:11.254 --> 01:22:13.415
It's like that's why it says,

01:22:13.415 --> 01:22:17.276
in the chorus it's like I'm trying to,

01:22:17.276 --> 01:22:20.074
essentially I'm trying to break the habit

01:22:20.074 --> 01:22:22.740
of knowing what life's all about, right?

01:22:22.740 --> 01:22:24.157
That's a tendency

01:22:25.284 --> 01:22:27.245
to, like, you gotta know
what life's all about

01:22:27.245 --> 01:22:30.876
and you gotta believe it
strongly and you gotta share it

01:22:30.876 --> 01:22:32.499
and you gotta, and it's
difficult to be like,

01:22:32.499 --> 01:22:33.334
"No, I can let go of that.

01:22:33.334 --> 01:22:35.082
I don't have to know

01:22:35.082 --> 01:22:37.731
what the ultimate purpose
of the universe is.

01:22:37.731 --> 01:22:39.063
And I believe that anyone who says

01:22:39.063 --> 01:22:40.729
that they've got it figured out

01:22:40.729 --> 01:22:43.388
almost assuredly is wrong."

01:22:43.388 --> 01:22:45.596
Like, the one thing I know
is if you say you know

01:22:45.596 --> 01:22:47.090
the secret of the universe is

01:22:47.090 --> 01:22:48.959
and what the purpose of all this is,

01:22:48.959 --> 01:22:51.415
whatever your particular take on it

01:22:51.415 --> 01:22:54.068
you're basically ruling that
out from being the truth

01:22:54.068 --> 01:22:55.859
because you've come up, you know,

01:22:55.859 --> 01:22:57.818
you and some system of
man has come up with that,

01:22:57.818 --> 01:22:58.982
from my perspective.

01:22:58.982 --> 01:23:01.899
I think when, from my experience,

01:23:03.032 --> 01:23:05.615
when love takes a close second,

01:23:06.645 --> 01:23:09.228
like even if it's a razor edge,

01:23:10.550 --> 01:23:14.800
like a thin slice just
below truth, it's a problem.

01:23:18.160 --> 01:23:22.121
It's a problem when you
start to relate to people,

01:23:22.121 --> 01:23:27.121
when you start to try to
empathize with people, you know.

01:23:27.640 --> 01:23:31.903
It's hard to love somebody
when there's subservience

01:23:31.903 --> 01:23:35.814
to what you believe to
be an absolute truth.

01:23:35.814 --> 01:23:37.897
And it always felt weird,

01:23:39.546 --> 01:23:42.804
and that's why, for so many years,

01:23:42.804 --> 01:23:46.887
I would hide, like, my
beliefs and my affiliation

01:23:48.894 --> 01:23:51.333
with the evangelical church,

01:23:51.333 --> 01:23:56.333
was because I just couldn't
stand by the implied judgment

01:23:56.554 --> 01:23:58.387
that was the headline.

01:23:59.235 --> 01:24:01.065
It's like too many people, I fear,

01:24:01.065 --> 01:24:05.482
would associate me with
judgment, and not love first.

01:24:08.705 --> 01:24:13.288
And I realized that, like,
I related to a lot of people

01:24:14.482 --> 01:24:18.815
for so much of my life,
with love being subservient,

01:24:19.872 --> 01:24:22.608
and people can feel that .

01:24:22.608 --> 01:24:23.899
They know it.
They know it.

01:24:23.899 --> 01:24:25.066
It sticks out like a sore thumb.

01:24:25.066 --> 01:24:27.149
Oh yeah, and it's like,

01:24:28.267 --> 01:24:32.829
yeah, I mean, that's one of
the big things that, you know,

01:24:32.829 --> 01:24:37.626
finally kinda broke me out
of that thinking was like,

01:24:37.626 --> 01:24:39.418
just like, it's not something to hide,

01:24:39.418 --> 01:24:42.048
it's something to break up away from.

01:24:42.048 --> 01:24:43.423
Well, you know, interesting,

01:24:43.423 --> 01:24:47.624
I've been thinking about this
exact concept a lot lately.

01:24:47.624 --> 01:24:49.707
And the way I've kind of,

01:24:50.732 --> 01:24:53.732
the way I understand it right now is

01:24:55.692 --> 01:25:00.606
if something about your
particular ideology or philosophy

01:25:00.606 --> 01:25:02.402
makes you necessarily think

01:25:02.402 --> 01:25:04.976
that someone is fundamentally
different, right?

01:25:04.976 --> 01:25:06.716
So, if you're a Christian,

01:25:06.716 --> 01:25:08.583
and if you're an evangelical Christian,

01:25:08.583 --> 01:25:10.367
and what I mean by that is if you believe

01:25:10.367 --> 01:25:13.871
that the only way to be in a
right relationship with God

01:25:13.871 --> 01:25:15.871
is through Jesus Christ,

01:25:16.748 --> 01:25:18.290
and so if you're not
in a right relationship

01:25:18.290 --> 01:25:20.867
with Jesus Christ, you are condemned,

01:25:20.867 --> 01:25:22.944
you're not really a child of God, right,

01:25:22.944 --> 01:25:25.552
you're gonna spend eternity in hell.

01:25:25.552 --> 01:25:29.552
If I believe that about
you, even if I love you,

01:25:30.575 --> 01:25:33.308
I think less of you by default.

01:25:33.308 --> 01:25:36.554
I think that I'm gonna
spend eternity with God

01:25:36.554 --> 01:25:38.130
and I think that you
are not going to spend

01:25:38.130 --> 01:25:39.377
eternity with God.

01:25:39.377 --> 01:25:40.747
Sure, it may be because of grace

01:25:40.747 --> 01:25:43.209
and it may be because of the gospel.

01:25:43.209 --> 01:25:44.908
It doesn't make you a bad
person to believe that,

01:25:44.908 --> 01:25:47.821
but the relational
dynamic that that creates

01:25:47.821 --> 01:25:49.781
is that this person who's
on the receiving end

01:25:49.781 --> 01:25:51.449
of that judgment, is what it is,

01:25:51.449 --> 01:25:54.782
it is a judgment, it is a determination,

01:25:56.871 --> 01:25:59.689
their relationship can
only be so deep with you.

01:25:59.689 --> 01:26:00.720
So, if you're a Christian

01:26:00.720 --> 01:26:04.002
and you think that I'm going to hell,

01:26:04.002 --> 01:26:08.427
again, you may be, that's
your theological opinion,

01:26:08.427 --> 01:26:10.089
you can't help yourself believe that,

01:26:10.089 --> 01:26:11.628
but I'm saying is that I can only have

01:26:11.628 --> 01:26:14.151
so deep of a relationship with you

01:26:14.151 --> 01:26:16.429
because there's a fundamental thing

01:26:16.429 --> 01:26:20.447
that you believe about
me that separates us

01:26:20.447 --> 01:26:24.019
and ultimately results
in me being discarded

01:26:24.019 --> 01:26:27.086
and judged in a way that
you're not going to be.

01:26:27.086 --> 01:26:30.239
And that's just a difficult dynamic,

01:26:30.239 --> 01:26:34.229
that is a result of fundamentalist
belief about things.

01:26:34.229 --> 01:26:36.509
But the moment you say,
"Hey, this is my take,"

01:26:36.509 --> 01:26:38.383
but I'm not really that certain about it

01:26:38.383 --> 01:26:42.792
and I'm not prepared to
say that you're condemned,

01:26:42.792 --> 01:26:45.916
the relational possibilities
really open up.

01:26:45.916 --> 01:26:47.425
That's a difficult thing to do, though.

01:26:47.425 --> 01:26:48.506
That's a very difficult thing to do

01:26:48.506 --> 01:26:51.589
if you hold to a particular theology.

01:26:52.594 --> 01:26:53.962
Where do we head next?

01:26:53.962 --> 01:26:55.745
So, this is the biggest departure,

01:26:55.745 --> 01:26:59.314
this is the last song that I wrote.

01:26:59.314 --> 01:27:00.977
And I wanted there to be

01:27:00.977 --> 01:27:03.508
what I would call a badass country song.

01:27:06.245 --> 01:27:08.501
And I'll explain what it means

01:27:08.501 --> 01:27:10.019
and why it's in this spot on the album

01:27:10.019 --> 01:27:11.053
after we listen to it.

01:27:11.053 --> 01:27:12.708
It's called "Kill a Man".

01:27:34.920 --> 01:27:39.920
♪ Today I'm gonna kill a man ♪

01:27:43.175 --> 01:27:48.175
♪ Gonna squeeze out his
life with my bare hands ♪

01:27:52.752 --> 01:27:57.752
♪ He's had it coming for way too long ♪

01:28:00.842 --> 01:28:05.675
♪ Only thing he does
right is doing wrong ♪

01:28:26.280 --> 01:28:29.049
You can just say it, man.

01:28:29.049 --> 01:28:30.799
Just freakin' say it.

01:28:32.334 --> 01:28:34.078
Say what?

01:28:34.078 --> 01:28:35.489
This was about me.

01:28:35.489 --> 01:28:37.241
I'm gonna kill you.

01:28:38.282 --> 01:28:39.991
This is me letting you know
that I'm going to kill you.

01:28:39.991 --> 01:28:42.478
This is a badass blues guitar.

01:28:42.478 --> 01:28:45.471
You got some Kenny Wayne Shepherd in here.

01:28:45.471 --> 01:28:47.215
Well, this is-

01:28:47.215 --> 01:28:49.211
You got the low notes,
you got the high notes.

01:28:49.211 --> 01:28:50.836
Well, here's the thing.

01:28:50.836 --> 01:28:54.495
This song was never going
to be a part of the album.

01:28:54.495 --> 01:28:56.491
I thought I was done with 10 songs.

01:28:56.491 --> 01:28:59.491
Then as we kept producing this thing

01:29:00.593 --> 01:29:03.015
and Alex coming through, and I was like,

01:29:03.015 --> 01:29:05.059
"This dude's really talented."

01:29:05.059 --> 01:29:06.136
Oh yeah, he did awesome.

01:29:06.136 --> 01:29:08.916
And I was like, I just feel
like I've gotta write a song

01:29:08.916 --> 01:29:10.742
that lets him just like,

01:29:10.742 --> 01:29:13.909
"Hey, buddy, I'm taking off the leash.

01:29:14.818 --> 01:29:17.466
I'm taking off the leash and
I just want you to be a badass

01:29:17.466 --> 01:29:21.572
on the guitar and just
do what you can do."

01:29:21.572 --> 01:29:25.239
And so thematically, I
mean, the message is,

01:29:26.925 --> 01:29:28.546
and I don't wanna, let me just say,

01:29:28.546 --> 01:29:30.167
I'm not gonna say exactly,

01:29:30.167 --> 01:29:35.023
there's a principle that was
true in my former philosophy

01:29:35.023 --> 01:29:39.968
as a Christian that is true
now as a whatever I am,

01:29:39.968 --> 01:29:44.823
hopeful agnostic, that I think
is an important principle

01:29:44.823 --> 01:29:47.323
in life that I still abide by.

01:29:48.728 --> 01:29:53.154
And this song is a sort of an
exploration of that concept.

01:29:53.154 --> 01:29:54.522
Okay.

01:29:54.522 --> 01:29:57.469
I'll let you figure out
exactly what that is.

01:29:57.469 --> 01:30:00.047
And thanks to Jessie for lending her-

01:30:00.047 --> 01:30:02.234
Operatic vocals.
Yeah, you know.

01:30:02.234 --> 01:30:04.859
She's done that for us
before back in the day.

01:30:04.859 --> 01:30:06.601
You know, Jessie majored in voice,

01:30:06.601 --> 01:30:08.924
specifically classical voice.

01:30:08.924 --> 01:30:10.960
So, I knew I wanted that
middle section to be like,

01:30:10.960 --> 01:30:12.501
kind of a weird departure

01:30:12.501 --> 01:30:15.381
and there's like some
backtrack guitars and stuff

01:30:15.381 --> 01:30:17.294
or whatever the term is, reverse.

01:30:17.294 --> 01:30:21.538
What are the backing vocals
that sound like a chant say?

01:30:21.538 --> 01:30:23.285
Are they words?

01:30:23.285 --> 01:30:24.118
Which part?

01:30:25.381 --> 01:30:27.080
It's like a percussive.

01:30:27.080 --> 01:30:29.385
Oh, they just say "yeah, yeah".

01:30:29.385 --> 01:30:30.989
They say "yeah, yeah"?
Yeah.

01:30:30.989 --> 01:30:32.589
Okay, all right.

01:30:32.589 --> 01:30:33.759
Glad we cleared that up.

01:30:33.759 --> 01:30:36.342
Yeah, it's a little bit of a,

01:30:39.565 --> 01:30:41.799
again, I don't think
there's Jack White song

01:30:41.799 --> 01:30:42.997
that sounds exactly like this,

01:30:42.997 --> 01:30:45.828
but you could imagine him
having done something like this.

01:30:45.828 --> 01:30:47.314
Sure, sure.
You know.

01:30:47.314 --> 01:30:50.843
And that's Derek doing that percussion.

01:30:50.843 --> 01:30:53.474
He just did it, like, outside
of his studio in Burbank,

01:30:53.474 --> 01:30:56.048
like on his driveway with, like, you know.

01:30:56.048 --> 01:30:57.716
Sounds like you've killed somebody

01:30:57.716 --> 01:31:00.035
and you're draggin' 'em to the-

01:31:00.035 --> 01:31:01.786
Well, it's kinda like you've got somebody

01:31:01.786 --> 01:31:04.358
and you got 'em in shackles

01:31:04.358 --> 01:31:07.305
and you're takin' 'em
to kill 'em, you know.

01:31:07.305 --> 01:31:09.599
Oh, that's morbid.

01:31:09.599 --> 01:31:10.818
You know, you could take
it however you want to,

01:31:10.818 --> 01:31:14.011
but that was what I was thinking.

01:31:14.011 --> 01:31:16.759
Now we get, final three songs,

01:31:16.759 --> 01:31:18.254
these two that I just put together.

01:31:18.254 --> 01:31:19.954
Originally, I wanted them to be spread out

01:31:19.954 --> 01:31:24.800
and then I was just like,
I've kinda set the stage to,

01:31:24.800 --> 01:31:28.633
these are my two songs
directed at the church.

01:31:30.705 --> 01:31:31.538
Okay.

01:31:31.538 --> 01:31:33.949
First one is called "In Vain".

01:31:36.948 --> 01:31:40.676
♪ There's quite a few things ♪

01:31:40.676 --> 01:31:44.182
♪ You don't seem to mind ♪

01:31:44.182 --> 01:31:47.522
♪ I'm trying to figure out ♪

01:31:47.522 --> 01:31:51.158
♪ Just where you draw the line ♪

01:31:51.158 --> 01:31:54.622
♪ 'Cause watching people die ♪

01:31:54.622 --> 01:31:58.118
♪ Seems to be fine ♪

01:31:58.118 --> 01:32:03.118
♪ But god forbid a nipple
or a Jesus Christ ♪

01:32:05.244 --> 01:32:08.831
♪ I sorta understand ♪

01:32:08.831 --> 01:32:12.248
♪ It took me quite a bit ♪

01:32:12.248 --> 01:32:16.002
♪ Before I'd let an oh my God ♪

01:32:16.002 --> 01:32:19.485
♪ Exit from my lips ♪

01:32:19.485 --> 01:32:22.543
♪ But maybe that commandment ♪

01:32:22.543 --> 01:32:26.603
♪ About using the Lord's name ♪

01:32:26.603 --> 01:32:30.769
♪ Is about a lot more than just ♪

01:32:30.769 --> 01:32:33.553
♪ What you say ♪

01:32:33.553 --> 01:32:38.553
♪ So much done in Jesus' name ♪

01:32:41.094 --> 01:32:46.094
♪ Seems to me to be a god damn shame ♪

01:32:47.507 --> 01:32:52.507
♪ Are you sure the Savior came ♪

01:32:55.492 --> 01:33:00.492
♪ So the righteous would
all vote the same ♪

01:33:01.203 --> 01:33:06.203
♪ Tell me who's the one
using His name in vain ♪

01:33:11.634 --> 01:33:13.374
See, I feel like there's gonna be

01:33:13.374 --> 01:33:17.652
a lot of people, Christians, who are like,

01:33:17.652 --> 01:33:20.350
"Hey, I agree with all of that, man.

01:33:20.350 --> 01:33:21.680
I agree with all of that."

01:33:21.680 --> 01:33:25.904
I think it's difficult,
though, in practice.

01:33:25.904 --> 01:33:29.824
I mean, I'm just saying
from personal experience,

01:33:29.824 --> 01:33:32.407
to go from yes, it's, you know,

01:33:36.497 --> 01:33:40.664
Jesus' teachings and the
biblical teaching can be,

01:33:41.728 --> 01:33:43.919
you know, we want to just boil it down

01:33:43.919 --> 01:33:48.287
to this actionable stuff
where we can check boxes,

01:33:48.287 --> 01:33:50.450
I know for me I wanted to
be able to check boxes,

01:33:50.450 --> 01:33:53.375
and say, "I'm well within bounds.

01:33:53.375 --> 01:33:57.235
I can feel secure about
my approach to life

01:33:57.235 --> 01:33:58.568
and with rules."

01:34:01.062 --> 01:34:04.312
And that's not how Jesus talked, right?

01:34:06.528 --> 01:34:10.435
And I was taught enough of that to know,

01:34:10.435 --> 01:34:15.257
to try to counteract it, but,
I mean, with my tendencies,

01:34:15.257 --> 01:34:19.795
I tended to really, when
you boil it down to rules,

01:34:19.795 --> 01:34:22.957
and even frivolous ones like,

01:34:22.957 --> 01:34:26.861
"Okay, don't say damn, but
definitely don't say goddamn,"

01:34:26.861 --> 01:34:31.429
and like, "Don't say,
'oh my God,'" you know,

01:34:31.429 --> 01:34:34.929
it's, like, it makes it easier to process,

01:34:36.763 --> 01:34:40.069
but none of this stuff
is that easy to process.

01:34:40.069 --> 01:34:42.903
It shouldn't be, you know.

01:34:42.903 --> 01:34:45.070
But as humans, I think we,

01:34:46.164 --> 01:34:51.164
at times you just fall back
on this reductionist approach.

01:34:51.554 --> 01:34:52.898
Well, I think it's, you know,

01:34:52.898 --> 01:34:56.600
I've always been fascinated
with how offensive

01:34:56.600 --> 01:34:58.603
using God's name in vain.

01:34:58.603 --> 01:35:01.103
Goddamn, saying goddamn is so,

01:35:02.071 --> 01:35:03.110
and this is something I tell people

01:35:03.110 --> 01:35:04.542
from other parts of the world

01:35:04.542 --> 01:35:08.618
and from other parts of
the United States like,

01:35:08.618 --> 01:35:12.704
