01:48:22.494 --> 01:48:24.395
this is kinda how I think about God.

01:48:24.395 --> 01:48:27.196
It feels like a breakup.

01:48:27.196 --> 01:48:29.391
You think about the
relationship that you had

01:48:29.391 --> 01:48:31.294
and you run things back through your mind

01:48:31.294 --> 01:48:33.204
and you're like, "What
was I actually thinking?

01:48:33.204 --> 01:48:34.109
What was going on?"

01:48:34.109 --> 01:48:37.283
But the reason, you know,
this is the last on the album

01:48:37.283 --> 01:48:41.217
is, you know, I am
committed to being open,

01:48:41.217 --> 01:48:44.121
like I have not closed
my accounts with reality,

01:48:44.121 --> 01:48:47.269
I have not closed my accounts with God.

01:48:47.269 --> 01:48:48.204
I'm not an atheist.

01:48:48.204 --> 01:48:49.301
And the reason that I
say I'm not an atheist

01:48:49.301 --> 01:48:51.884
is because I lean into the idea

01:48:54.128 --> 01:48:56.557
that there's something else
going on here, you know.

01:48:56.557 --> 01:48:57.613
I don't know what that is.

01:48:57.613 --> 01:48:58.462
I don't know what the nature of that is.

01:48:58.462 --> 01:48:59.801
I don't know what the nature of God is.

01:48:59.801 --> 01:49:01.865
I don't know what the
nature of the universe is.

01:49:01.865 --> 01:49:04.988
But I don't know what it is,

01:49:04.988 --> 01:49:08.638
and so therefore I'm not
ready to conclude what it is.

01:49:08.638 --> 01:49:12.819
And it may be that I hadn't
got the first time around

01:49:12.819 --> 01:49:15.289
when I was in a relationship with God,

01:49:15.289 --> 01:49:18.284
as I would've described
it, I had Him wrong,

01:49:18.284 --> 01:49:21.324
I had her wrong, I had it
wrong, I had them wrong,

01:49:21.324 --> 01:49:23.310
whatever you want, however you want.

01:49:23.310 --> 01:49:26.274
And who knows what the
future holds, right?

01:49:26.274 --> 01:49:29.072
If the last 20 years of
my life indicate anything,

01:49:29.072 --> 01:49:31.057
it's that the next 20 years could send me

01:49:31.057 --> 01:49:34.461
in a wildly different direction

01:49:34.461 --> 01:49:36.690
than I would've ever anticipated.

01:49:36.690 --> 01:49:39.446
And so the last thing I wanna
do is close myself off to,

01:49:39.446 --> 01:49:43.196
if that God out there,
in there, around there

01:49:46.702 --> 01:49:48.688
wants to be in relationship

01:49:48.688 --> 01:49:52.528
and wants to reveal some
kind of truth to me,

01:49:52.528 --> 01:49:54.845
I'm not gonna be the
one to stand in the way

01:49:54.845 --> 01:49:56.191
of that God doing that.

01:49:56.191 --> 01:49:59.195
And so I leave it open and unresolved.

01:49:59.195 --> 01:50:01.885
I enjoyed the production technique

01:50:01.885 --> 01:50:04.702
of, like, the pan vocals.

01:50:04.702 --> 01:50:07.619
But is there irony in the fact that

01:50:08.495 --> 01:50:11.479
your processing of God's letters

01:50:11.479 --> 01:50:14.729
is, like, a conversation with yourself?

01:50:15.776 --> 01:50:19.667
So, there's a Jason
Isbell song that I love,

01:50:19.667 --> 01:50:21.574
"Chaos and Clothes" I
think is the name of it,

01:50:21.574 --> 01:50:25.138
and he does this technique of
basically doubling his voice

01:50:25.138 --> 01:50:28.299
and the stereo split of the two voices.

01:50:28.299 --> 01:50:29.922
And I knew I wanted to do a song like this

01:50:29.922 --> 01:50:32.070
and I thought that this song
was a perfect one to do it

01:50:32.070 --> 01:50:36.330
because it just represents
the possibility.

01:50:36.330 --> 01:50:37.630
To me, it's not some,

01:50:37.630 --> 01:50:39.576
it is interesting that
you take it that way,

01:50:39.576 --> 01:50:40.796
which I'll take that.

01:50:40.796 --> 01:50:41.728
I'm not taking credit for it,

01:50:41.728 --> 01:50:45.496
but, like, is this just a
conversation with myself?

01:50:45.496 --> 01:50:47.036
This is why this is the
only song on the album

01:50:47.036 --> 01:50:48.739
where I did my own
harmony 'cause I was like,

01:50:48.739 --> 01:50:51.045
this is so personal, that it's like,

01:50:51.045 --> 01:50:53.153
so there's two melodies
and there's two harmonies

01:50:53.153 --> 01:50:58.153
and there's two guitars and
it's just, like, represents

01:50:58.263 --> 01:51:00.535
the multiple perspectives and how,

01:51:00.535 --> 01:51:04.103
where we go from here is
something that is just kinda,

01:51:04.103 --> 01:51:08.195
it's open, the possibilities
are open and I don't know,

01:51:08.195 --> 01:51:11.923
I'm not trying to go any particular place.

01:51:11.923 --> 01:51:15.488
Well, you know, I celebrate the fact

01:51:15.488 --> 01:51:18.729
that this thing exists, it's out there,

01:51:18.729 --> 01:51:20.273
it's a complete project.

01:51:20.273 --> 01:51:25.179
I mean, I know, with everything
that you've been through

01:51:25.179 --> 01:51:28.747
and how the pandemic, in a lot of ways,

01:51:28.747 --> 01:51:30.489
gave you the space to make this possible,

01:51:30.489 --> 01:51:33.000
there's still a lot of
stuff that's going on

01:51:33.000 --> 01:51:33.931
and more every day.

01:51:33.931 --> 01:51:36.529
It's, like, it's nice
that you followed through

01:51:36.529 --> 01:51:38.235
on this vision and this is out there

01:51:38.235 --> 01:51:40.985
and, you know, it'll outlive you.

01:51:44.811 --> 01:51:46.105
You mean it's still
gonna be on the internet

01:51:46.105 --> 01:51:47.863
after I'm dead.
Oh yeah.

01:51:47.863 --> 01:51:48.719
That's great.

01:51:48.719 --> 01:51:52.742
I mean, as a project,
I think it's important

01:51:52.742 --> 01:51:54.730
and I celebrate it.

01:51:54.730 --> 01:51:55.824
Well, thanks for that,

01:51:55.824 --> 01:51:59.271
and thanks for listening to it with me.

01:51:59.271 --> 01:52:03.688
You know, I think that the
most rewarding part so far

01:52:04.784 --> 01:52:08.431
has been the people who, and,
again, when I record this-

01:52:08.431 --> 01:52:10.797
The horrible cover songs?

01:52:10.797 --> 01:52:12.911
There's been a lot of great cover songs.

01:52:12.911 --> 01:52:15.019
There's only two tracks out at
the time that I record this,

01:52:15.019 --> 01:52:17.660
you know, the first two singles.

01:52:17.660 --> 01:52:18.835
But the most rewarding thing,

01:52:18.835 --> 01:52:20.456
and again this is what I
said from the beginning,

01:52:20.456 --> 01:52:24.468
if one person is like,
"Ah, I relate to this,"

01:52:24.468 --> 01:52:27.555
like, you wrote a theme
song for my life situation

01:52:27.555 --> 01:52:31.207
or what I've been through
in this relationship,

01:52:31.207 --> 01:52:32.825
that's been the most rewarding thing,

01:52:32.825 --> 01:52:37.565
is people giving that
feedback and saying that like,

01:52:37.565 --> 01:52:41.915
"You put into words what I've
been feeling and thinking

01:52:41.915 --> 01:52:44.798
and haven't been able to say," you know.

01:52:44.798 --> 01:52:47.756
And, again, I talked about,

01:52:47.756 --> 01:52:52.756
part of the inspiration for
this album being David Bazan,

01:52:52.977 --> 01:52:56.477
Pedro the Lion, and specifically his album

01:52:58.167 --> 01:53:02.706
"Curse the Branches", which
obviously different musically

01:53:02.706 --> 01:53:03.602
and different thematically,

01:53:03.602 --> 01:53:05.348
but it was like a deconstruction album

01:53:05.348 --> 01:53:07.131
from way back in the day,

01:53:07.131 --> 01:53:11.308
when I was in the very
beginnings of my deconstruction.

01:53:11.308 --> 01:53:13.581
And it was just so, like, I kept,

01:53:13.581 --> 01:53:15.000
I listened to that album so many times

01:53:15.000 --> 01:53:18.486
'cause I was like, "Man,
people," I just didn't,

01:53:18.486 --> 01:53:19.579
there wasn't enough music out there

01:53:19.579 --> 01:53:22.948
that resonated with specifically
this very difficult thing

01:53:22.948 --> 01:53:25.418
that I was dealing with.

01:53:25.418 --> 01:53:26.509
And more people are doing it.

01:53:26.509 --> 01:53:27.765
There's more deconstruction.

01:53:27.765 --> 01:53:29.546
Again, I hate to use the word.

01:53:29.546 --> 01:53:33.075
It's overused, but it's
the best descriptor.

01:53:33.075 --> 01:53:34.943
There's more and more music that connects

01:53:34.943 --> 01:53:36.808
with everybody who's
experiencing this stuff.

01:53:36.808 --> 01:53:38.472
I just wanted to do my
part to get it out there.

01:53:38.472 --> 01:53:40.472
So thanks for listening.

01:53:41.596 --> 01:53:42.895
Do you happen to have a rec?

01:53:42.895 --> 01:53:44.145
It's your week.

01:53:45.532 --> 01:53:48.488
I guess if I have to search deeply,

01:53:48.488 --> 01:53:53.238
I would say that I recommend
that you listen to my album.

01:53:54.228 --> 01:53:56.868
"Human Overboard" is
the name of the album.

01:53:56.868 --> 01:53:59.343
James and the Shame is
the name of the artist.

01:53:59.343 --> 01:54:00.828
That's me.

01:54:00.828 --> 01:54:04.644
And it is available
everywhere you stream music.

01:54:04.644 --> 01:54:06.750
And I will ask, you know, it's funny,

01:54:06.750 --> 01:54:09.341
I don't know what it'll be like
by the time this comes out,

01:54:09.341 --> 01:54:14.119
but I see, like, on Spotify,
like, people also like

01:54:14.119 --> 01:54:15.545
and everybody who they also like

01:54:15.545 --> 01:54:18.714
is, like, another internet
famous person who does music.

01:54:18.714 --> 01:54:21.019
And so I guess it's inevitable
that that's what the case is.

01:54:21.019 --> 01:54:22.746
But I would just say, "Hey, listen,

01:54:22.746 --> 01:54:26.786
if this album was your
introduction to country

01:54:26.786 --> 01:54:29.774
or Americana, hey, there's a lot

01:54:29.774 --> 01:54:31.465
of really good stuff out there," you know.

01:54:31.465 --> 01:54:32.942
I've put together that
playlist a while back

01:54:32.942 --> 01:54:34.241
that was, like, some of my inspirations.

01:54:34.241 --> 01:54:36.846
Listen to that music, not
only for your own benefit

01:54:36.846 --> 01:54:37.838
because there's a lot of great

01:54:37.838 --> 01:54:39.394
country music that's out there,

01:54:39.394 --> 01:54:41.087
but also it'll start to show up

01:54:41.087 --> 01:54:43.812
that people also like country music.

01:54:45.070 --> 01:54:46.595
Not Ninja Sex Party.

01:54:46.595 --> 01:54:49.940
Nothing against Ninja Sex
Party, we love those guys,

01:54:49.940 --> 01:54:51.503
but I don't think that Ninja Sex Party

01:54:51.503 --> 01:54:54.890
is the greatest entry point
into James and the Shame.

01:54:54.890 --> 01:54:57.273
No, it's not.

01:54:57.273 --> 01:54:59.804
So, do some listening
to train the algorithm,

01:54:59.804 --> 01:55:01.059
if you don't mind.

01:55:01.059 --> 01:55:04.141
All right, as always, you can
call us, leave us voicemail,

01:55:04.141 --> 01:55:05.316
